@page "/components/commandpalette"
@page "/components/command-palette"

<PageOutlet Url="components/commandpalette"
            Title="CommandPalette"
            Description="BitCommandPalette is the Ctrl+K box that fuzzy-searches every page, action and setting of an app and runs the one picked." />

<DemoPage Name="CommandPalette"
          SecondaryNames="@(["CommandMenu", "Cmdk", "Spotlight"])"
          Description="BitCommandPalette is an accessible, keyboard-first command palette: the Ctrl+K (Cmd+K on a Mac) box that fuzzy-searches every page, action and setting of an app and runs the one picked, with nested pages, prompts, multi-select, async sources, recents and global shortcuts. Matching, ranking and every decision run in C#; the browser only shows the native dialog and listens for keys."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="CommandPalette/BitCommandPalette.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Extras/CommandPalette/BitCommandPaletteDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            BitCommandPalette is the Ctrl+K (Cmd+K on a Mac) box that fuzzy-searches every page, action and setting of an app
            and runs the one picked - accessible, keyboard-first, with every decision made in C#. Its registry, open state and
            history are services of their own, registered by <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices()</code> in Program.cs (in both
            projects of a Blazor Web App). The sections below go from the basics - a palette in markup, the setup, the hosting
            models, the modal palette and its trigger - to the full API.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                A command palette is the one place in an application where everything can be reached by typing: the pages to go to,
                the actions to run, the settings to change, records fetched from an API, multi-step flows such as "rename", "assign to"
                or "set labels", and the keyboard shortcuts that go with them. It is the pattern of VS Code's Quick Pick, Linear, GitHub,
                Raycast and cmdk, built natively for Blazor: matching, ranking, paging, history and what <b>Enter</b> does are all
                decided in C#, and the browser only shows the native dialog, moves the focus, scrolls the active row into view and
                listens for keys - there is no JavaScript command-menu library underneath.
                <br />
                <br />
                The simplest palette declares its commands in markup: a <b>BitCommandPaletteGroup</b> per heading, holding a
                <b>BitCommandPaletteOption</b> per command. <b>Title</b> is what is searched first and highlighted, <b>Keywords</b>
                (separated by commas or spaces) are other words that find it, <b>Subtitle</b> is a second line, <b>Badge</b> a small
                label, and <b>Disabled</b> with <b>DisabledReason</b> keeps a command listed but refuses to run it and says why.
                <br />
                <br />
                Type to search: the matcher is fzf-style fuzzy matching, so "gs" finds "<b>G</b>o to <b>s</b>ettings" - it rewards word
                starts, camelCase humps and consecutive runs, ignores case unless the query has a capital, and highlights the characters
                it matched. "options" finds "Go to settings" through its keywords. Move with the arrow keys (they wrap around), Home and
                End, Page Up and Page Down; run the active command with <b>Enter</b> or a click. Escape clears the query of an inline
                palette.
                <br />
                <br />
                This palette is <b>Inline</b>: it is part of the page and always open, for a settings search, a docs search page or a
                command list in a side panel. <b>IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"</b> makes it list only its own commands, not the ones
                registered app-wide with <b>IBitCommandPaletteService</b> (the next section is about those).
                <b>OnCommandExecuted</b> reports every command it ran.
                <br />
                <br />
                The sections below add the rest: the modal Ctrl+K palette and its trigger, commands built in C#, nested pages, toggles,
                prompts and confirmations, global shortcuts, modes by prefix ("&gt;", "#", "@@", "?"), async sources against your own API,
                multi-select, recents and pins, guards that check a permission every time a command is about to run, templates, theming
                and localization.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" OnCommandExecuted="e => aLastRun = e.Command.Title">
                    <BitCommandPaletteGroup Heading="Navigation">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Go to home" Keywords="start, dashboard" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Go to settings" Keywords="preferences, options" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Go to profile" Subtitle="Your account" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteGroup>
                    <BitCommandPaletteGroup Heading="Actions">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Create a document" Badge="New" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Invite a teammate" Keywords="share, add people" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Archive the project" Disabled DisabledReason="Only owners can archive" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteGroup>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@aLastRun</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Getting started" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                BitCommandPalette ships in Bit.BlazorUI.Extras. Set that package up as the Getting started page of this site describes: install it, call <b>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices()</b>, and link the <b>bit.blazorui.extras.css</b> stylesheet and the <b>bit.blazorui.extras.js</b> script in the host page. The palette's styles and its small browser layer are inside those two files, so nothing else is linked for it. Then four steps wire it up.
                <br />
                <br />
                <b>1. Configure it</b>, if the defaults do not suit, through the <b>commandPalette</b> argument of <b>builder.Services.AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices(commandPalette: options =&gt; ...)</b>, which sets <b>BitCommandPaletteOptions</b> (more toggle shortcuts, the order of the groups, startup commands, modes and sources). That call already registers the palette's services, so there is nothing else to register. The options are a singleton. The registry, the open state and the history are scoped: one per circuit on Blazor Server, one per application on WebAssembly.
                <ul>
                    <li>In a Blazor Web App, call it in both the server and the client project, through one extension method that both Program.cs files share.</li>
                    <li>Both are needed because every page is prerendered on the server before it runs in the browser, so whatever a component injects, such as IBitCommandPaletteService, must resolve in both containers.</li>
                    <li>A palette running in a project that did not call AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices uses the default options and ignores yours, and it logs a warning that says so.</li>
                    <li>A WebAssembly app that lazy loads Bit.BlazorUI.Extras skips this step, as the Hosting models section explains.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>2. Place one palette</b>, a <b>&lt;BitCommandPalette /&gt;</b>, where it is rendered interactively on every page - usually the main layout - and a <b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b> wherever a visible entry point helps. The palette renders a closed <b>&lt;dialog&gt;</b> until it is opened, so it costs nothing while it is not in use. It needs an interactive render mode, because a statically rendered palette never attaches its keyboard handling. Place one modal palette per scope: every instance answers the same service, so two of them would open together.
                <br />
                <br />
                <b>3. Register commands</b> wherever they belong, by injecting <b>IBitCommandPaletteService</b> and calling <b>Register</b>. It returns an <b>IDisposable</b>: dispose it to remove the commands again, so commands registered by a page leave with the page. Commands that belong to the whole app are registered once, from the layout or at startup through <b>options.Commands</b>, and <b>&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommands Commands="..." /&gt;</b> registers a list while it is rendered and removes it when it goes. The sections of this page cover everything a command can be.
                <br />
                <br />
                <b>4. Open it.</b> Ctrl+K (Cmd+K on a Mac) toggles the palette. Code opens it through <b>OpenAsync</b> of the service, optionally with a query (a mode prefix included) or directly on a command's page, and <b>ExecuteAsync</b> runs a command by its id without opening anything.
                <br />
                <br />
                <b>Requirements.</b>
                <ul>
                    <li>It needs .NET 10 or .NET 11.</li>
                    <li>It runs on Blazor WebAssembly, Blazor Server, a Blazor Web App in any interactive render mode, and Blazor Hybrid.</li>
                    <li>It needs a browser with the dialog element, which every evergreen browser has had since 2022.</li>
                    <li>It is trimming and AOT friendly.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>How it fits together.</b> A key press is first seen by the small browser layer in bit.blazorui.extras.js, which handles the toggle, global chords, G-then-X sequences and the navigation keys, and hands over only what belongs to the palette - so typing on the page never costs a round trip. Everything after that runs in C#:
                <ul>
                    <li>BitCommandPalette keeps the query, the mode prefix, the stack of pages and the active row.</li>
                    <li>BitCommandPaletteQuery parses the query into terms and operators, once per keystroke.</li>
                    <li>IBitCommandPaletteMatcher scores every visible command's fields, fzf style, and returns the positions to highlight.</li>
                    <li>BitCommandPaletteRanker combines that score with priority and frecency and keeps the best MaxResults.</li>
                    <li>The groups, pinned and recent commands and async source blocks are flattened into one list of rows, so keyboard navigation, aria-activedescendant, scrolling and virtualization all work on a single model.</li>
                    <li>The native dialog shows them in the top layer, traps the focus and points aria-activedescendant at the active row.</li>
                </ul>
                What matches, in which order, what Enter does and which page comes next are decided in C#, so they can be unit tested, replaced and reasoned about like the rest of the app. A wrapped JavaScript command menu would keep the commands, the matching and the flows on the other side of the interop boundary and reach every handler back across it. Here commands are typed <b>BitCommandPaletteCommand</b> records whose handlers are C# delegates running in the user's DI scope, a multi-step flow is a handler returning the next step (a prompt, a page, a toast, back), and an API is an <b>IBitCommandPaletteSource</b> that is debounced and cancelled through a CancellationToken.
                <ul>
                    <li>The model is BitCommandPaletteCommand and its fluent builder BitCommandPaletteCmd, with results, contexts, modes and keyboard shortcuts; it is immutable and free of UI.</li>
                    <li>Search is BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher, BitCommandPaletteSubstringMatcher, the field weights, BitCommandPaletteRanker and the async sources; it is pure C# that runs in a unit test.</li>
                    <li>The service is IBitCommandPaletteService: the registry, the open state and the events, one per circuit or browser tab.</li>
                    <li>History is recents, pins and frecency over a pluggable IBitCommandPaletteHistoryStore.</li>
                    <li>The components are BitCommandPalette, BitCommandPaletteTrigger, BitCommandPaletteGroup, BitCommandPaletteOption, BitCommandPaletteCommands, BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint and BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText.</li>
                </ul>
                An AI agent writing palette code can ask the bit BlazorUI MCP server (https://blazorui.bitplatform.dev/mcp) for this component's API, these samples and the setup guide of each hosting model, instead of guessing.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>The code below is the whole setup of an app; this page is set up the same way, and its one modal palette is in the Modal section.</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Hosting models" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                The palette works in every Blazor hosting model; what differs is where the services are registered, where the palette is placed and where its work runs.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Blazor WebAssembly</b>: register the services in Program.cs, link the Extras stylesheet and script in index.html, and place the palette in MainLayout.razor. Searching runs on the browser's one thread, so a keystroke whose search took longer than a frame paints first, and keys typed meanwhile fold into one search; typing stays responsive with thousands of commands.</li>
                    <li><b>Blazor Server</b>: the setup is the same. Shortcuts and navigation keys are handled in the browser, so only the query and the chosen command cross the circuit, and handlers run on the server in the circuit's scope. Keys typed faster than a round trip are all kept, and the search runs at once instead of deferring to the next frame, because the network already separates typing from rendering.</li>
                    <li><b>Blazor Web App</b>: register the services in both the server and the client project, and link the stylesheet and script in App.razor. With global interactivity (<code>&lt;Routes @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</code>, InteractiveWebAssembly or InteractiveAuto) the palette goes in MainLayout.razor.</li>
                    <li>With per-page interactivity the layout is static, so wrap the palette in a small component that the layout renders with an interactive mode, such as <code>&lt;PaletteHost @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</code>, and register commands from components that run in the same mode, because the registry is scoped to one circuit or one WebAssembly runtime.</li>
                    <li>While prerendering, the palette renders a closed dialog; once it is interactive and its browser layer listens for keys, it marks the dialog with data-ready="true", which is what an end-to-end test waits for before pressing Ctrl+K on a prerendered page.</li>
                    <li><b>Blazor Hybrid</b>: the palette works unchanged in a MAUI, WPF or Windows Forms BlazorWebView, set up as on WebAssembly, and its history goes to the web view's localStorage.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Lazy loading on WebAssembly.</b> The palette, its trigger and BitCommandPaletteCommands also work without AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices: when the services are not registered, they create every service they need themselves, once per browser connection, and <b>IServiceProvider.GetBitCommandPaletteService()</b> hands the app's own code the same IBitCommandPaletteService. So a WebAssembly app can download Bit.BlazorUI.Extras only when someone opens a part of the app that has a palette: mark Bit.BlazorUI.Extras.wasm as lazy loaded in the project that runs in the browser, load it in the router's OnNavigateAsync before the first page that uses it renders, and let those pages, or a layout only they use, hold the palette and register commands through GetBitCommandPaletteService() instead of injecting IBitCommandPaletteService.
                <ul>
                    <li>Leave AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices out of that project's Program.cs and register the core services with AddBitBlazorUIServices() instead. Calling it would reference the Extras types before the assembly is there; the palette then runs on the default options.</li>
                    <li>For the same reason, only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use its types: the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not App.razor, a layout every page shares or Program.cs. The other Extras components are then available on those pages only.</li>
                    <li>No type in an assembly the router reads when the app starts, the app's own assembly first of all, may derive from or implement one of the palette's types: no IBitCommandPaletteSource class, no IBitCommandPaletteHistoryStore and no component that inherits BitCommandPalette. On WebAssembly a type whose base type or interface lives in an assembly that is not loaded yet fails to load, and keeps failing after the assembly has loaded, for the rest of the session. Put such a class in a library that is lazy loaded together with Bit.BlazorUI.Extras.</li>
                    <li>The stylesheet and the script are plain link and script tags, served whether the assembly has loaded or not.</li>
                    <li>Without the registration the palette runs on the default BitCommandPaletteOptions. Its parameters override most of them per instance (Strings, ToggleShortcut, Loop, EnableHistory and others), a trigger shows and announces the toggle shortcut and the strings of the palette it opens, and modes, sources and commands are registered through GetBitCommandPaletteService() instead of options.Modes, options.Sources and options.Commands.</li>
                    <li>The remaining options, such as GroupOrder, AdditionalToggleShortcuts, the field weights, the matcher and AllowedUrlSchemes, keep their defaults; an app that needs them registers the services rather than lazy loading.</li>
                    <li>The history store cannot be replaced either: recents, pins and frecency go to the browser's localStorage under the default HistoryStorageKey, bit-command-palette.history, which every app on the same origin shares. An app that must not write to the browser sets EnableHistory="false" on its palettes.</li>
                    <li>The palette logs one warning that the services are not registered. That is expected here; it is what tells an app that should have registered them, such as the client project of a Blazor Web App that forgot to, why its options are ignored. A logging filter on the category Bit.BlazorUI.BitCommandPaletteServiceCollectionExtensions silences it without referencing the palette's types.</li>
                    <li>Do not lazy load in an AOT build: AOT compiles every assembly into dotnet.native.wasm, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Content security policy.</b> The palette's browser layer is part of _content/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras/scripts/bit.blazorui.extras.js, loaded by its script tag. The palette adds no inline script and no eval, so script-src 'self' is enough for it; the rest of the policy is what Blazor itself needs, which on WebAssembly includes 'wasm-unsafe-eval'.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>This site is a Blazor Web App: the palette in the Modal section runs in whichever render mode the site was opened in, prerendered first.</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Modal" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                The modal palette is the Ctrl+K box: a native modal <b>&lt;dialog&gt;</b> that opens over the page, sits in the top layer,
                makes the page behind it inert, holds the page's scroll still and keeps the focus inside until it closes. An app places
                <b>one</b> of it, in an interactively rendered layout, and fills it from everywhere: besides the commands passed to its
                <b>Commands</b> parameter and the ones declared in its markup, it lists every command, mode and source registered through
                <b>IBitCommandPaletteService</b> (<b>IncludeRegisteredCommands</b> is true by default). Here "Wave goodbye" is registered
                through the injected service; every later section of this page that registers something shows up in this palette too.
                Until it is opened the dialog renders nothing inside, so it costs nothing while it is not in use. One modal palette per
                scope is the rule: every instance answers the same service, so two of them would both open.
                <br />
                <br />
                It opens in four ways:
                <ul>
                    <li>its toggle shortcut, <b>Ctrl+K</b> (<b>Cmd+K</b> on a Mac), from anywhere on the page - pressed again, it closes the palette;</li>
                    <li>a <b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b>, a button showing that shortcut (the next section covers it);</li>
                    <li>binding <b>Open</b>: <b>@@bind-Open</b> both opens and closes it, and follows the palette whichever way it was opened or closed;</li>
                    <li><b>paletteService.OpenAsync()</b> from code - with a query already typed (<b>OpenAsync("hello")</b>; a mode prefix
                        such as ">" opens that mode) or straight on a command's page (<b>OpenAsync(pageCommandId: "a-greetings")</b>).
                        <b>CloseAsync()</b> and <b>ToggleAsync()</b> drive it the same way, and <b>ExecuteAsync(id)</b> runs a command as if it
                        had been picked, without opening anything unless the command needs the palette.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Escape</b> steps back one level at a time: it first disarms a destructive command waiting for its confirmation, then
                goes back from a nested page ("Choose a greeting" is one; Backspace on an empty query goes back too), and only then
                closes the palette. Closing gives the focus back to whatever had it - the trigger or the button that opened it. A click
                on the backdrop around the dialog closes it as well; an app that does not want that sets
                <b>options.CloseOnBackdropClick = false</b> in the <b>commandPalette</b> argument of <b>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</b>. When a command runs the palette closes,
                unless the command answers with a next step (a page, a prompt, a toast that stays).
                <br />
                <br />
                <b>ToggleShortcut</b> replaces the toggle shortcut of this palette (the default comes from
                <b>options.ToggleShortcut</b>, "Mod+K", where Mod is Cmd on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere); turn on the switch to make it Alt+K
                and watch the trigger follow. <b>options.AdditionalToggleShortcuts</b> adds more, e.g. VS Code's "Mod+Shift+P". Inside the
                palette its own keys win over a toggle bound to the same keys. <b>OnOpened</b> and <b>OnClosed</b> are raised after the
                palette opened and closed, however it happened; <b>IBitCommandPaletteService.Opened</b> and <b>Closed</b> report the same
                to code anywhere else.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger />
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => aModalOpen = true">Open by binding Open</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="AOpenWithQuery">Open with "hello" typed</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="ARunSayHello">Run "Say hello" by its id</BitButton>
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitToggle Label="Toggle with Alt+K instead of Ctrl+K" @bind-Value="aAltToggle" />
                <BitCommandPalette @bind-Open="aModalOpen"
                                   Commands="aModalCommands"
                                   ToggleShortcut="@aToggleShortcut"
                                   OnOpened="() => aOpenedCount++"
                                   OnClosed="() => aClosedCount++" />
                <br />
                <BitText>
                    Open: <b>@aModalOpen</b>, opened <b>@aOpenedCount</b> and closed <b>@aClosedCount</b> times, last run: <b>@aModalLastRun</b>
                </BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Trigger" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b> is the palette's visible way in: a native button that opens the page's modal palette and
                shows its toggle shortcut in the platform's own symbols - Cmd K on a Mac, Ctrl K elsewhere, once the palette has detected
                the platform - and declares it to assistive technology with <b>aria-keyshortcuts</b>. The palette is one keystroke away on
                every page, but someone who has not learnt the keystroke yet needs something to click, so a header usually holds one. It
                follows the modal palette it opens: its hint is that palette's <b>ToggleShortcut</b> (switch the Modal section's palette
                to Alt+K and every trigger here changes) and its words are that palette's <b>Strings</b>.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Label</b> is its text; the default is "Search", and an empty one shows the icon alone. Its accessible name starts
                        with the label it shows and goes on to say what it does - "Search the docs…, Open command palette" - so someone who
                        says "click Search the docs" with voice control reaches it (WCAG 2.5.3, label in name). The words come from
                        <b>BitCommandPaletteStrings.OpenPalette</b> and <b>TriggerNameSeparator</b>; a label that already says it all is
                        the whole name, and an <b>AriaLabel</b> overrides both.</li>
                    <li><b>Query</b> opens the palette with a query already typed - a search, or a mode prefix such as ">" that opens that
                        mode (modes come later on this page).</li>
                    <li><b>PageCommandId</b> opens the palette directly on the page of the command with that id - here the "Choose a
                        greeting" page of the Modal section's palette.</li>
                    <li><b>ShowShortcut="false"</b> drops the shortcut hint.</li>
                    <li><b>ChildContent</b> replaces the whole content - icon, label and hint. The trigger cannot read the text of content
                        you give it, so name it: content that shows only an icon sets <b>Label=""</b> and the button is named "Open command
                        palette"; content that shows a word sets <b>Label</b> to that word and is named as the default trigger is; content
                        with no <b>Label</b> is named by what it shows followed by a visually hidden ", Open command palette".</li>
                    <li><b>MacKeys</b> forces one platform's symbols - true for Cmd, false for Ctrl - instead of the detected one, e.g. for
                        a screenshot or a page about one platform.</li>
                </ul>
                Like every BlazorUI component it takes <b>Class</b>, <b>Style</b>, <b>Disabled</b> and any other attribute (it lands on the
                button); <b>Classes</b>/<b>Styles</b> reach its parts (Root, Icon, Label, Shortcut), and the
                <b>--bit-CommandPaletteTrigger-*</b> variables restyle its defaults. Without a modal palette on the page a trigger has
                nothing to open.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Search the docs…" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Find a greeting" Query="greet" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Choose a greeting" PageCommandId="a-greetings" ShowShortcut="false" />
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="">
                        <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.Search" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteTrigger>
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Greetings" Query="greet">
                        <b>Greetings</b>
                    </BitCommandPaletteTrigger>
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Query="hello">
                        Say <b>hello</b>
                    </BitCommandPaletteTrigger>
                </div>
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Mac keys" MacKeys="true" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Windows and Linux keys" MacKeys="false" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Commands from code" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                A command is an immutable <code>BitCommandPaletteCommand</code>: a title, where it is listed, what it matches and what
                it does. <code>BitCommandPaletteCmd</code> starts one and every method of the <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandBuilder</code>
                it returns sets one member; a builder converts to a command on its own, so a list of them needs no <code>Build()</code>
                calls - only a target that is not a <code>BitCommandPaletteCommand</code> does.
                <br />
                The entry points: <code>BitCommandPaletteCmd.New("Export…")</code> starts a blank command;
                <code>Navigate("Settings", "/settings")</code> sets <code>Href</code> and makes it a navigation;
                <code>Action("Save", () =&gt; Save())</code> runs an <code>Action</code> and closes the palette;
                <code>Action("Reload", ReloadAsync)</code> does the same with a <code>Func&lt;Task&gt;</code>;
                <code>Action("Rename…", ctx =&gt; ...)</code> takes a <code>Func&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandContext, Task&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandResult&gt;&gt;</code>
                and so decides what happens next; <code>Page("Theme", light, dark)</code> makes a page of the commands it is given.
                <code>Handler</code> has the same overloads: an <code>Action</code>, an <code>Action&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandContext&gt;</code>
                or a <code>Func&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandContext, Task&gt;</code> close the palette when they are done, while a handler
                returning a <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult</code> says what comes next (stay open with <code>KeepOpen</code>, go
                <code>Back</code>, a page, a prompt, a toast or an error - see the sections on handlers and results).
                <br />
                Every member of the model, with the builder method that sets it:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Id</code> - stable identity; derived from the title, group and parent when not set (<code>.Id(...)</code>, see Identity). <code>HasExplicitId</code> says whether it was set.</li>
                    <li><code>Title</code> - the label, required; searched with the highest weight and highlighted (<code>.Title(...)</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>Subtitle</code> - a secondary label next to the title, such as a path or a category; searched and highlighted.</li>
                    <li><code>Description</code> - longer text for the detail pane; searched with a low weight.</li>
                    <li><code>Keywords</code> - aliases, synonyms and abbreviations (<code>.Keywords("download", "save as")</code>); a keyword hit is shown under the title.</li>
                    <li><code>Group</code> - the heading the command is listed under.</li>
                    <li><code>Icon</code> / <code>IconCss</code> - a <code>RenderFragment</code> (<code>.Icon(fragment)</code>) or a CSS class for an icon font (<code>.Icon("bit-icon bit-icon--Save")</code>); without either, the row shows its kind's icon.</li>
                    <li><code>Shortcut</code> / <code>GlobalShortcut</code> - a <code>BitCommandPaletteKeyboardShortcut</code>, a chord (<code>Mod+S</code>) or a sequence (<code>G S</code>); global by default, so it fires while the palette is closed, and <code>.Shortcut("Mod+S", global: false)</code> only shows it.</li>
                    <li><code>Priority</code> - a ranking nudge; <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandPriority</code> names <code>Lowest</code> (-100), <code>Low</code> (-50), <code>Normal</code> (0, the default), <code>High</code> (50) and <code>Highest</code> (100), and any other number works too.</li>
                    <li><code>Parent</code> - the id of the command whose page lists this one; set for you on static children.</li>
                    <li><code>Children</code> / <code>ChildrenProvider</code> / <code>ChildSources</code> - nested commands: static (<code>.Children(a, b)</code>), loaded when the page opens (<code>.Children(async (ctx, ct) =&gt; ...)</code>), or searched as the page is typed in (<code>.Source(...)</code>). <code>HasChildren</code> says whether it opens a page.</li>
                    <li><code>ChildrenPlaceholder</code> / <code>MultiSelectChildren</code> - the search box placeholder on that page, and whether its rows are checkboxes (<code>.MultiSelect()</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>Actions</code> - secondary actions listed in the action panel on <code>Tab</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>Handler</code> - what runs on selection: <code>Func&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandContext, Task&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandResult&gt;&gt;</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>Href</code> / <code>ForceLoad</code> / <code>OpenInNewTab</code> - a navigation target, used when there is no handler (<code>.Href(url, forceLoad: false, newTab: true)</code>); only relative links and the allowed schemes are followed.</li>
                    <li><code>Kind</code> - <code>Action</code>, <code>Navigation</code>, <code>Toggle</code>, <code>Setting</code>, <code>Search</code>, <code>Page</code>, <code>Help</code> or <code>Recent</code>: picks the default icon and is exposed as <code>data-kind</code>. <code>Href</code>, <code>Children</code>, <code>Value</code> and <code>Checked</code> set it for you, so call <code>.Kind(...)</code> after them to override it.</li>
                    <li><code>Disabled</code> / <code>DisabledReason</code> - listed but not runnable; the reason replaces the subtitle (<code>.Disabled(true, "Requires the Pro plan")</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>IsVisible</code> - a predicate evaluated every time the list is built (<code>.VisibleWhen(() =&gt; ...)</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>Guard</code> - asked every time the command is about to run whether it may (<code>.Guard(ctx =&gt; ..., "message")</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>Destructive</code> / <code>ConfirmMessage</code> - drawn in the danger colour; with a message, <code>Enter</code> has to be pressed twice (<code>.Destructive("...")</code>, or <code>.Confirm("...")</code> for the second <code>Enter</code> alone).</li>
                    <li><code>Badge</code> / <code>Value</code> / <code>Checked</code> - a pill ("New", "3"), a setting's current value ("Dark"), or a toggle's switch.</li>
                    <li><code>AlwaysShow</code> - listed whatever the query, like cmdk's <code>forceMount</code>, for a "couldn't find it?" row.</li>
                    <li><code>ExcludeFromHistory</code> - never recorded in recents or frecency.</li>
                    <li><code>Tags</code> - free-form labels that modes can scope to.</li>
                    <li><code>Data</code> - anything your templates or handlers need.</li>
                </ul>
                Commands are immutable on purpose: they are shared between the registry, the rows and the history, and a change is a
                new command with the same id. <code>command.With(b =&gt; b.Title("Rename…").Badge("New"))</code> derives one.
                <br />
                There are three ways to hand commands to the palettes, and they can all be used at once: the palette's
                <code>Commands</code> parameter (a list of this one palette), <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.Register(...)</code> (the
                scoped registry every palette of the scope lists), and markup (see Declarative markup). <code>Register</code> takes the
                commands as a <code>params</code> array or an <code>IEnumerable</code> and returns an <code>IDisposable</code>: the
                commands are in the registry - and in the modal palette, shortcuts included - from the moment they are registered until
                that handle is disposed. Keep the handle and dispose it when the component that registered them goes.
                <br />
                Below, an inline palette lists the commands passed to its <code>Commands</code> parameter: a blank command, actions
                built from an <code>Action</code>, a <code>Func&lt;Task&gt;</code> and a handler that keeps the palette open, a page
                (Backspace goes back), a navigation and a link that opens in a new tab, and a help row with a badge and an inline SVG
                icon. The button registers <i>Say hello</i> with the service, so the modal palette lists it, and disposes the handle on
                the next click.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="bCommands" />
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="BToggleHello">@(bHello is null ? "Register \"Say hello\"" : "Dispose the registration")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("hello")'>Search "hello" in the modal palette</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@bLastRun</b> · "Say hello" is <b>@(paletteService.Find(BHelloId) is null ? "not registered" : "registered")</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Identity" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                Recents, pins, the active row, <code>Find</code>, <code>Update</code>, <code>Unregister</code>, <code>ExecuteAsync</code>
                and <code>OpenAsync(pageCommandId: ...)</code> all go by a command's <code>Id</code>. Without one it is derived: a
                lower-case slug of the title (every character that is not a letter or a digit becomes a dash), prefixed with the parent's
                id and the group, joined by dots - <code>Settings</code> in the group <code>Navigation</code> is
                <code>navigation.settings</code>, and <code>Dark</code> under the page <code>preferences.theme</code> is
                <code>preferences.theme.dark</code>. A static child registered with the service gets its parent's id as that prefix, and a
                <code>BitCommandPaletteOption</code> derives its id the same way from its <code>Title</code>, its group's
                <code>Heading</code> and the option it is nested in. A title made of symbols only, such as <code>🚀</code>, derives its id
                from its code points (<code>u1f680</code>). The table below is worked out by the library on this page, right now.
                <br />
                Set the id explicitly whenever the title can change - a toggle whose label says "Enable" or "Disable", a translated title -
                so recents, pins and the active row survive the change:
                <code>BitCommandPaletteCmd.New(wordWrap ? "Disable word wrap" : "Enable word wrap").Id("toggle:wordwrap")</code>.
                A copy made with <code>With</code> keeps its id even when its title changes.
                <br />
                Different titles can derive the same id - <code>C#</code> and <code>C++</code> are both <code>c</code> - and then one
                replaces the other. When two commands registered together share an id, the service logs a warning (through
                <code>ILogger</code>) naming both titles; give them explicit ids.
                <br />
                Registering a command with an id that is already registered replaces it: the later registration wins, which lets a page
                override a global command of the layout (and its shortcut) while it is shown, and the earlier one comes back when the
                override's registration is disposed. Inside one palette there is one command per id: the registry's, then the one
                declared in its markup, then the one passed to its <code>Commands</code> parameter.
                <br />
                Below, <i>Greet the team</i> is registered with the id <code>b-greet</code> when the page starts. The button registers a
                second command with the same id; while that registration lives, <code>Find("b-greet")</code> and the modal palette
                return the override, and disposing it brings the first one back.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <table class="b-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Built as</th><th>Id</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var (built, id) in bIdentityExamples)
                        {
                            <tr><td><code>@built</code></td><td><code>@id</code></td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="BToggleGreetOverride">@(bGreetOverride is null ? "Register an override with the same id" : "Dispose the override")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("greet")'>Search "greet" in the modal palette</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitText>Find("b-greet"): <b>@(paletteService.Find(BGreetId)?.Title)</b> · Last run: <b>@bGreetStatus</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Groups and ordering" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <code>Group</code> puts a command under a heading. On the empty query the palette lists, in this order: pinned and recent
                commands (when history is on), then the ungrouped commands - under a heading of their own, <i>Suggestions</i>, when pinned
                or recent ones are listed above them, so they do not read like more recents - then the groups named in
                <code>BitCommandPaletteOptions.GroupOrder</code>, in that order, then every other group: the group holding the
                highest-priority (or most used) command first, alphabetically on a tie.
                <br />
                Inside a group, commands are ordered by <code>Priority</code>, then by frecency, then in the order they were registered or
                declared - list <i>Light</i>, <i>Dark</i> and <i>Auto</i> and that is the order they appear in. <code>Priority</code> is a
                number; <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandPriority</code> names <code>Lowest</code> (-100), <code>Low</code> (-50),
                <code>Normal</code> (0, the default), <code>High</code> (50) and <code>Highest</code> (100). It leads on the empty query,
                and only nudges a search, where the match decides.
                <br />
                While searching, results are ordered by rank (equal ranks by title), and the groups by their best match.
                <code>GroupWhenSearching = false</code> drops the headings while a query is typed and lists the results as one ranked list
                instead. It is an app-wide option (<code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices(commandPalette: options =&gt; options.GroupWhenSearching = false)</code>)
                that the palette's <code>GroupWhenSearching</code> parameter overrides for one palette. <code>GroupOrder</code> is only an
                option, set once for the app.
                <br />
                The palette below lists the same commands this page would without a <code>GroupOrder</code>: <i>Search everywhere</i> has
                no group and comes first; <i>Edit</i> holds a <code>High</code> command (<i>Undo</i>), so it leads the groups, and
                <i>Undo</i> leads it; <i>Help</i> and <i>View</i> tie and follow alphabetically; <i>Reset zoom</i> is <code>Low</code> and
                <i>Send feedback</i> <code>Lowest</code>, so both sink to the end of their group. <i>Send feedback</i> is also
                <code>AlwaysShow</code>: type anything and it is still there. <code>EnableHistory="false"</code> keeps recents out of this
                palette, so the order you see is only the one described here. Type "o" and flip the toggle to compare a grouped and a flat
                search.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="bGroupWhenSearching" Label="Group the results while searching" />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   GroupWhenSearching="bGroupWhenSearching"
                                   Commands="bOrderingCommands" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Toggles and settings" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                A toggle shows a switch and a setting shows its current value: <code>.Checked(state)</code> makes a command a
                <code>Toggle</code>, <code>.Value("Dark")</code> makes it a <code>Setting</code>, and a setting with children opens a page of
                its choices. Both usually answer <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult.KeepOpen</code>, so the change can be seen in the
                palette, and a choice on a page answers <code>Back</code>, so the setting shows its new value. Give such commands an
                explicit <code>Id</code>, so the active row, recents and pins survive the change of their state or label.
                <br />
                Because commands are immutable, the new state is a new command. There are three ways to hand it over:
                <ul>
                    <li>Build the list again and pass the palette a new collection: its <code>Commands</code> parameter is re-read when it
                        is handed a new instance (a collection changed in place needs <code>paletteService.Refresh()</code>). That is what the
                        inline palette below does - rebuilding a few records is cheap.</li>
                    <li>Re-register it: a <code>BitCommandPaletteCommands</code> handed a new collection swaps its registration (see
                        Page-scoped commands).</li>
                    <li>Call <code>paletteService.Update(command)</code>, which replaces the command with the same id wherever it was
                        registered - its static children included - or adds it when no command has that id. It suits a registry too large to
                        rebuild, and a label that changes with the state.</li>
                </ul>
                The inline palette below and the controls above it show the same state: change it on either side and the other follows.
                <i>Enter focus mode</i> is registered with the service (so the modal palette lists it); every time it runs - from the modal
                palette or from the button - it is replaced through <code>paletteService.Update</code> with a command of the same id
                <code>b-focus-mode</code>, titled <i>Leave focus mode</i> and checked, and back again.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="bWordWrap" @bind-Value:after="BBuildSettings" Label="Word wrap" />
                    <BitToggle @bind-Value="bMinimap" @bind-Value:after="BBuildSettings" Label="Show minimap" />
                    @foreach (var theme in bEditorThemes)
                    {
                        <BitButton Variant="@(bEditorTheme == theme ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)" OnClick="() => BSetEditorTheme(theme)">@theme</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="bSettings" />
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="BToggleFocusMode">@(bFocusMode ? "Leave focus mode" : "Enter focus mode") from the page</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("focus")'>Search "focus" in the modal palette</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitText>Registered as <code>b-focus-mode</code>: <b>@(paletteService.Find(BFocusModeId)?.Title)</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Disabled, hidden and destructive commands" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
            <div>
                Three ways to say a command cannot, or should not, simply run:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Disabled, with a reason</b>: <code>.Disabled(true, "Requires the Pro plan")</code> lists the command but does
                        not run it. The reason replaces the subtitle, and <code>Enter</code> on the row answers with it in the footer.
                        Use it when the user should learn that the command exists and why it is not available.</li>
                    <li><b>Hidden until it applies</b>: <code>.VisibleWhen(() =&gt; selection.Count &gt; 1)</code> sets
                        <code>IsVisible</code>, a predicate evaluated every time the list is built, so an open palette is always right.
                        The global shortcuts, though, are synced to the browser ahead of time: when state a predicate reads changes while
                        the palette is closed - a selection, the signed-in user, the current route - call <code>paletteService.Refresh()</code>.
                        The command that just became available then answers its shortcut, and the one that went away leaves its keys to the
                        page. Registering, updating or removing a command refreshes on its own. A row chosen after its predicate (or an
                        ancestor's) turned false since the list was built - the user signed out in another tab and nobody called
                        <code>Refresh()</code> - does not run: the footer says "“Merge selected files” is no longer available."
                        (<code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.CommandUnavailable</code>) and the list is rebuilt.</li>
                    <li><b>Destructive, confirmed</b>: <code>.Destructive("This permanently deletes the draft")</code> draws the command in
                        the danger color, and with a message the first <code>Enter</code> arms it and shows the message, the second runs it;
                        <code>Esc</code> or moving to another row disarms it. <code>.Confirm("message")</code> asks for the second
                        <code>Enter</code> without the danger color, and <code>.Destructive()</code> without a message only colors the row.
                        A command run from code with <code>ExecuteAsync</code> that needs a confirmation opens the palette to ask.</li>
                </ul>
                Below, <i>Merge selected files</i> is registered with the service with the shortcut <code>Alt+M</code> and
                <code>.VisibleWhen(() =&gt; bSelectedFiles.Count &gt;= 2)</code>, and listed by the inline palette too. Select two files and
                it is there - in both palettes and on the keyboard; select one and it is gone from all three, because every click calls
                <code>paletteService.Refresh()</code>. <i>Export as PDF</i> is disabled with its reason, <i>Publish the draft</i> asks for a
                second <code>Enter</code>, and <i>Delete the draft</i> is destructive (and <code>Low</code> priority, so the danger zone comes
                last). Nothing here deletes anything.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    @foreach (var file in bFiles)
                    {
                        <BitButton Variant="@(bSelectedFiles.Contains(file) ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)" OnClick="() => BToggleFile(file)">@file</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <BitText>
                    Selected: <b>@(bSelectedFiles.Count == 0 ? "nothing" : string.Join(", ", bFiles.Where(bSelectedFiles.Contains)))</b> ·
                    "Merge selected files" is <b>@(bSelectedFiles.Count >= 2 ? "listed, and Alt+M runs it" : "hidden, and Alt+M is left to the page")</b>
                </BitText>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="bAvailabilityCommands" />
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("merge")'>Search "merge" in the modal palette</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@bAvailabilityStatus</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Page-scoped commands" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
            <div>
                Commands that only make sense on one page are registered by that page and go with it.
                <code>&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommands Commands="..." /&gt;</code> renders nothing: it registers its <code>Commands</code> with
                <code>IBitCommandPaletteService</code> while it is rendered and disposes the registration when it is - leave the page and its
                commands are gone from the palette, shortcuts included (kbar calls this <code>useRegisterActions</code>). Handed a new
                collection instance, it swaps the registration, so assigning a new list after a state change is enough to show the new
                commands; the same instance changed in place is not noticed. It works even where <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</code>
                was never called. <code>paletteService.Register(...)</code> does the same from code and hands back the
                <code>IDisposable</code> to dispose.
                <br />
                A <code>Shortcut</code> is global by default: the modal palette - the one a layout places once - claims it on the whole
                document, so it runs its command while the palette is closed, as long as the page that registered it is shown. An inline
                palette never claims shortcuts. When several commands claim the same keys, the one registered last wins (the rule ids
                follow too), so a page's own <code>Mod+S</code> takes over from the layout's while the page is shown.
                <br />
                A registered command is changed in place with <code>paletteService.Update</code>: <code>Find</code> the current one, derive
                a copy with <code>With</code> - same id, handler and shortcut - and hand it over. The registration
                <code>BitCommandPaletteCommands</code> holds is edited in place, so disposing it when the page goes still removes the copies.
                <br />
                Below, <i>Increment counter</i> and <i>Decrement counter</i> are registered by this page. Open the modal palette and look for
                them under "Counter", or press <code>Alt+↑</code> / <code>Alt+↓</code> anywhere on the page with the palette closed. Both
                keep the palette open, so the value can be stepped from inside it, and both carry the count as a badge, updated through
                <code>Update</code> on every change. <i>Reset counter</i> is destructive, so it asks first, and it is disabled, with its
                reason, while the counter is at zero.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPaletteCommands Commands="bPageCommands" />
                <BitText>Counter: <b>@bCount</b></BitText>
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("counter")'>Search "counter" in the modal palette</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => BSetCount(bCount + 1)">+1 from the page</BitButton>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Declarative markup" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
            <div>
                Commands can also be written in markup, as children of the palette, next to the component that owns them.
                <code>BitCommandPaletteGroup</code> gives its <code>Heading</code> (required) to the options inside it, its
                <code>Priority</code> to every option that does not set its own, and adds its <code>Tags</code> to theirs.
                <code>BitCommandPaletteOption</code> has a parameter for most members of the model:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Title</code> (required), <code>Id</code>, <code>Subtitle</code>, <code>Description</code>, and
                        <code>Keywords</code> - one comma or space separated string.</li>
                    <li><code>Icon</code> (a <code>RenderFragment</code>, written as an <code>&lt;Icon&gt;</code> child element) or
                        <code>IconCss</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>Shortcut</code> (<code>Mod+Shift+D</code>, <code>G I</code>) and <code>GlobalShortcut</code> (default
                        <code>true</code>; <code>false</code> only shows the keys).</li>
                    <li><code>Href</code> and <code>NewTab</code>: an option with an <code>Href</code> and no <code>OnExecute</code>
                        navigates.</li>
                    <li><code>OnExecute</code> and <code>KeepOpen</code>: <code>OnExecute</code> receives the
                        <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandContext</code>, and the palette closes afterwards unless <code>KeepOpen</code> is set
                        (an inline palette goes back to its root page instead).</li>
                    <li><code>Priority</code>, <code>Kind</code> (inferred when not set), <code>Tags</code>, <code>AlwaysShow</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>Disabled</code> and <code>DisabledReason</code>; <code>Destructive</code> and <code>ConfirmMessage</code> - a
                        message alone asks for the second <code>Enter</code> without the danger color.</li>
                    <li><code>Badge</code>, <code>Value</code> and <code>Checked</code>.</li>
                    <li>Nested options as its child content turn it into a page, with <code>ChildrenPlaceholder</code> for its search box;
                        <code>MultiSelect</code> makes that page's rows checkboxes - <code>Enter</code> or <code>Space</code> checks one,
                        <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> (<code>Cmd+Enter</code>) calls the parent's <code>OnExecute</code> with the checked ones in
                        <code>SelectedCommands</code>.</li>
                </ul>
                An option re-registers only when one of its parameters actually changed, so re-rendering a layout full of them is cheap;
                bind <code>Checked</code> or <code>Value</code> to state and the row follows it.
                <br />
                What markup cannot say needs the builder: <code>Data</code>, <code>VisibleWhen</code>, <code>Guard</code>,
                <code>ExcludeFromHistory</code>, <code>Actions</code>, <code>ForceLoad</code>, children loaded when the page opens, a page
                that searches a source, and a handler that answers with a prompt, a page, a toast or an error rather than closing or
                staying open. Pass such commands to the palette's <code>Commands</code> parameter, or register them with the service.
                <br />
                Where a palette's commands come from: the service's registry, shared by every palette in the scope; the markup inside that
                palette, which belongs to that palette alone; and its <code>Commands</code> parameter, a third, per-instance list. A
                palette with <code>IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"</code> - like every inline one on this page - lists only the last two.
                <br />
                Below, every parameter of both components in one palette: a group with a priority and tags, a page of choices, a
                multi-select page, a toggle and a setting that keep the palette open, a confirmation, a destructive and a disabled option,
                links, and an always-shown row with its own icon.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false">
                    <BitCommandPaletteGroup Heading="Quick create" Priority="20" Tags="@(new[] { "create" })">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="New issue"
                                                 Id="b-new-issue"
                                                 Subtitle="In this project"
                                                 Description="Opens the new issue form."
                                                 Keywords="create, add bug"
                                                 Shortcut="Alt+N"
                                                 GlobalShortcut="false"
                                                 IconCss="bit-icon bit-icon--Add"
                                                 OnExecute='_ => bMarkupLastRun = "New issue"' />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="New…" ChildrenPlaceholder="What do you want to create?">
                            <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Project" OnExecute='_ => bMarkupLastRun = "New project"' />
                            <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Document" Badge="Beta" OnExecute='_ => bMarkupLastRun = "New document"' />
                        </BitCommandPaletteOption>
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Set labels…" MultiSelect ChildrenPlaceholder="Check labels, Ctrl+Enter to apply…" OnExecute="BApplyLabels">
                            <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Bug" Id="b-label-bug" />
                            <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Feature" Id="b-label-feature" />
                            <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Docs" Id="b-label-docs" />
                        </BitCommandPaletteOption>
                    </BitCommandPaletteGroup>
                    <BitCommandPaletteGroup Heading="Preferences">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Word wrap" Checked="bMarkupWrap" KeepOpen OnExecute="() => bMarkupWrap = !bMarkupWrap" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Font size" Value="@($"{bMarkupFontSize}px")" KeepOpen OnExecute="() => bMarkupFontSize = bMarkupFontSize >= 18 ? 12 : bMarkupFontSize + 2" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Publish" ConfirmMessage="Publish to everyone?" OnExecute='_ => bMarkupLastRun = "Published (not really)"' />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Clear the cache" Destructive ConfirmMessage="Clear the cache? Everything is downloaded again." OnExecute='_ => bMarkupLastRun = "Cleared the cache (not really)"' />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Sync now" Disabled DisabledReason="You are offline" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteGroup>
                    <BitCommandPaletteGroup Heading="Links">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Getting started" Href="/getting-started" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="bit platform on GitHub" Href="https://github.com/bitfoundation/bitplatform" NewTab Kind="BitCommandPaletteCommandKind.Help" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteGroup>
                    <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Couldn't find it?"
                                             AlwaysShow
                                             Priority="BitCommandPaletteCommandPriority.Lowest"
                                             OnExecute='ctx => bMarkupLastRun = $"Feedback: \"{ctx.Query}\""'>
                        <Icon>
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.5" /><path d="M6.2 6.2a1.9 1.9 0 1 1 2.6 1.8c-.5.2-.8.6-.8 1.1v.4M8 11.6v.1" /></svg>
                        </Icon>
                    </BitCommandPaletteOption>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@bMarkupLastRun</b> · Word wrap: <b>@bMarkupWrap</b> · Font size: <b>@bMarkupFontSize px</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Inline palettes" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
            <div>
                <code>Inline</code> renders the palette in the page rather than over it - a settings search, a docs search page, a list of
                commands in a side panel - with the same search, keyboard and templates. Set it before the first render; changing it later
                is not supported. An inline palette is a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> named like the dialog
                (<code>data-bit-cpl="inline"</code>, class <code>bit-cpl-inl</code>), always open and as wide as its container;
                <code>--bit-CommandPalette-max-height</code> on its <code>Style</code> bounds its list. To make it denser, set
                <code>--bit-layout-density-scale</code> (0.8, say) on its <code>Style</code> or an ancestor, and
                <code>ShowFooter="false"</code> drops the footer and its key hints.
                <br />
                How it behaves in a page:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Esc</code> disarms a confirmation, goes back a page or clears the query instead of closing; with nothing to
                        undo it is left to the page, so a drawer or a dialog around the palette still closes on it.</li>
                    <li><code>Tab</code> moves the focus on, as everywhere else on a page: there is no focus trap, no backdrop and no scroll
                        lock.</li>
                    <li>A command that would close the palette resets it to its root page, and result counts are announced only while it has
                        the focus.</li>
                </ul>
                It stays out of the way of the modal palette a layout usually has too: it neither opens on the toggle shortcut nor answers
                <code>OpenAsync</code>, <code>CloseAsync</code> or <code>ExecuteAsync</code>, and it claims no global shortcuts (it still
                shows them), so nothing runs twice. A key the modal palette claimed - <code>Ctrl+K</code> typed in the inline palette's box -
                is left to it. <code>IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"</code> leaves out the registry's commands, sources and modes, so the
                inline palette lists only what is declared in its markup or passed to <code>Commands</code>; leave it <code>true</code> for
                a search page that should find everything the modal palette finds.
                <br />
                Below, an inline palette in a side panel. Open <i>Go to symbol</i> and press <code>Esc</code>: the palette goes back. Type
                and press <code>Esc</code>: it clears the query. Press <code>Esc</code> once more with nothing to undo: the panel, which
                closes on <code>Escape</code>, closes. <i>Format document</i> shows <code>Alt+Shift+F</code>, but pressing it does nothing,
                because an inline palette claims no shortcuts; <code>Ctrl+K</code> typed in its box opens the modal palette.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                @if (bInlinePanelOpen)
                {
                    <div class="b-inline-panel" @onkeydown="BInlinePanelKeyDown">
                        <div class="b-inline-panel-header">
                            <b>Side panel</b>
                            <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" OnClick="() => bInlinePanelOpen = false">Close</BitButton>
                        </div>
                        <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                           IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                           ShowFooter="false"
                                           Placeholder="Search this panel's commands…"
                                           Style="--bit-CommandPalette-max-height: 260px; --bit-layout-density-scale: 0.85;"
                                           Commands="bInlineCommands" />
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => bInlinePanelOpen = true">Reopen the side panel</BitButton>
                }
                <br />
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@bInlineStatus</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="The service" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
            <div>
                <code>IBitCommandPaletteService</code> is the palette's programmatic surface, registered (scoped) by
                <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</code> and injected wherever it is needed. Its members:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Register(params BitCommandPaletteCommand[])</code> / <code>Register(IEnumerable&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommand&gt;)</code> -
                        add commands, their static children included; dispose the returned <code>IDisposable</code> to remove them.</li>
                    <li><code>Update(command)</code> - replace the command with the same id wherever it was registered, or add it.</li>
                    <li><code>Unregister(id)</code> - remove one command by its id, nested ones included; returns whether it removed one.</li>
                    <li><code>Find(id)</code> - look a registered command up by its id, nested ones included; <code>null</code> when there is none.</li>
                    <li><code>Refresh()</code> - re-evaluate the <code>VisibleWhen</code> predicates for the global shortcuts and an open list
                        after application state changed.</li>
                    <li><code>RegisterMode(mode)</code>, <code>RegisterSource(source)</code> - add a mode or a default-mode async source at
                        runtime; dispose the result to remove it (see the sections on modes and sources).</li>
                    <li><code>OpenAsync(query, pageCommandId)</code>, <code>CloseAsync()</code> (which cancels a command still running),
                        <code>ToggleAsync()</code> - drive the modal palette; a query may carry a mode prefix and operators, and
                        <code>pageCommandId</code> opens it directly on a command's page.</li>
                    <li><code>ExecuteAsync(id)</code> - run a command as if it had been chosen - one from the registry, or one declared in the
                        modal palette's markup or passed to its <code>Commands</code> - opening the palette when it needs it (a page, a
                        confirmation, a prompt). Guards are asked first. It throws <code>KeyNotFoundException</code> for an unknown id, and
                        rethrows what the handler threw unless a <code>CommandFailed</code> subscriber set <code>Handled</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>IsOpen</code>, <code>IsMac</code> (true once the palette detected a macOS or iOS browser - render ⌘ instead of
                        Ctrl from it), <code>Commands</code>, <code>Modes</code>, <code>Sources</code> - state.</li>
                    <li>Events: <code>CommandsChanged</code> (the registry changed), <code>Opened</code>, <code>Closed</code>,
                        <code>CommandExecuted</code> (the command, its <code>Result</code> and the <code>Duration</code> of its handler),
                        <code>CommandFailed</code> (the command and the <code>Exception</code>; a subscriber can change the <code>Message</code>
                        the palette shows, or set <code>Handled</code> to say it reported the error itself, which also stops the palette
                        logging it and <code>ExecuteAsync</code> rethrowing it), <code>CommandRefused</code> (a guard refused a command: the
                        command, the <code>Trigger</code> - <code>Palette</code>, <code>Shortcut</code> or <code>Programmatic</code> - and the
                        <code>Message</code>; useful because a refused shortcut or <code>ExecuteAsync</code> runs while the palette is
                        closed), <code>PlatformDetected</code> (re-render shortcut hints drawn from <code>IsMac</code>) and
                        <code>SequencePending</code> (the first chords of a sequence were pressed, then <code>null</code> when it completed,
                        broke off or timed out).</li>
                </ul>
                The service outlives a page: subscribe named handlers, not lambdas, and remove them in the page's <code>Dispose</code>.
                <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code> draws a shortcut as key caps; pass it <code>MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac"</code>.
                Where the services were not registered (an app that lazy loads the palette on WebAssembly),
                <code>IServiceProvider.GetBitCommandPaletteService()</code> returns the one the palette uses, and the registered one
                otherwise, so code that has to work both ways can always call it. The <code>BitCommandPalette</code> component itself
                exposes the same <code>OpenAsync</code>, <code>CloseAsync</code> and <code>ToggleAsync</code>, plus <code>SetQueryAsync</code>,
                <code>Query</code> and <code>ActiveCommand</code>, the two-way bindable <code>Open</code> parameter, and the
                <code>OnCommandExecuted</code>, <code>OnQueryChanged</code>, <code>OnOpened</code> and <code>OnClosed</code> callbacks.
                <br />
                Below, the buttons drive the service and the log lists its events, newest first. <i>Enter focus mode</i> runs without opening
                the palette; <i>Sync with the server</i> fails on purpose (its <code>CommandFailed</code> subscriber replaces the message and
                marks it handled); <i>Transfer ownership</i> has a guard that always refuses; <i>Scratch note</i> is removed with
                <code>Unregister</code> and registered again. Open the palette, run commands from it, or start a sequence to see the rest.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => paletteService.ExecuteAsync(BFocusModeId)">ExecuteAsync("b-focus-mode")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => paletteService.ExecuteAsync(BFailId)">ExecuteAsync("b-sync")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => paletteService.ExecuteAsync(BRefusedId)">ExecuteAsync("b-transfer")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="BToggleScratch">@(paletteService.Find(BScratchId) is null ? "Register \"Scratch note\" again" : "Unregister(\"b-scratch-note\")")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick='() => paletteService.OpenAsync("service")'>Open the modal palette</BitButton>
                </div>
                <BitText>
                    The palette is <b>@(paletteService.IsOpen ? "open" : "closed")</b> ·
                    IsMac: <b>@paletteService.IsMac</b>, so the toggle reads <BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="bToggleShortcut" MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac" /> ·
                    the registry holds <b>@paletteService.Commands.Count</b> commands ·
                    Find("b-focus-mode"): <b>@(paletteService.Find(BFocusModeId)?.Title ?? "null")</b> ·
                    Find("b-scratch-note"): <b>@(paletteService.Find(BScratchId)?.Title ?? "null")</b>
                </BitText>
                <br />
                <div class="b-event-log" role="log" aria-label="Service events">
                    @if (bEvents.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <div>Open the palette or run a command.</div>
                    }
                    @foreach (var entry in bEvents)
                    {
                        <div>@entry</div>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Handlers and results" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
            <div>
                A handler does its work and then tells the palette what comes next by returning a <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult</code>.
                That is what keeps a multi-step flow in one place: the next step is a return value, not a callback wired up somewhere else.
                <code>BitCommandPaletteCmd.Action(title, () =&gt; ...)</code> and the <code>Handler</code> overloads that take an <code>Action</code>
                or return a plain <code>Task</code> answer <b>Close</b>; give <code>Handler</code> a
                <code>Func&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandContext, Task&lt;BitCommandPaletteCommandResult&gt;&gt;</code> to pick another result:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Close</b>: closes the palette. The default of every handler that returns no result, and of navigation. An inline palette, which never closes, goes back to its root page with the query cleared instead.</li>
                    <li><b>KeepOpen</b>: stays open on the same page and rebuilds the list - what toggles use. A command is an immutable record, so a toggle's <code>Checked</code> is fixed on it: build the commands again from your state after the change (or <code>paletteService.Update</code> a registered one) and the rebuilt list shows the switch's new state.</li>
                    <li><b>Back</b>: goes back one page, staying open - the options of a settings page use it to land on the list again. On the root page (an option found from the root) it stays there and rebuilds the list.</li>
                    <li><b>Reset</b>: clears the query and returns to the root page, staying open - for a flow that is finished but whose user is likely to want the palette again.</li>
                    <li><b>Toast(message, keepOpen)</b>: shows a message in the footer, then closes shortly afterwards - unless <code>keepOpen</code> is true, or the palette is used again meanwhile. <code>Esc</code> while that close is pending is the close itself.</li>
                    <li><b>Error(message)</b>: shows an error, announced assertively, and stays where the palette is - on a prompt, so the value can be corrected. It is for the failures a handler expects (a name already taken, a plan that does not allow it); exceptions are for the ones it does not, see Errors below.</li>
                    <li><b>Page(title, commands, placeholder, multiSelect, onSubmit)</b>: pushes a page listing <code>commands</code>. They are not registered anywhere: they live on that page only, which is what a list the handler decides when it runs needs. With <code>multiSelect: true</code>, <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> runs <code>onSubmit</code> with the checked ones (see Multi-select).</li>
                    <li><b>Prompt(title, onSubmit, validate, placeholder, initialValue)</b>: turns the search box into a text field and runs <code>onSubmit</code> with what was typed (see Prompts).</li>
                </ul>
                Try each row below; each subtitle names the result its handler returns. "Add a to-do…" chains two of them: a prompt, then
                <b>Reset</b>, back on the root where the new to-do is listed first; <code>Enter</code> on a to-do completes it and keeps the palette open.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cResultCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitText>@cResultStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="The command context" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example16CsharpCode" Id="example16">
            <div>
                Every handler receives a <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandContext</code>, which carries what it may need to decide and to act:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Command</b>: the command being run - its <code>Id</code>, <code>Title</code>, <code>Data</code> and <code>Tags</code>, so one handler can serve many commands.</li>
                    <li><b>Query</b>: what was typed when it ran, without a mode prefix; <b>ModePrefix</b> is that prefix (<code>&gt;</code>, <code>#</code>...), or null in the default mode. A "couldn't find it?" row thanks the user for the very query it was reached with.</li>
                    <li><b>PromptValue</b>: the text of a prompt step, in the <code>onSubmit</code> of a <code>Prompt</code> result.</li>
                    <li><b>SelectedCommands</b>: the checked rows of a multi-select page, when its handler runs on <code>Ctrl+Enter</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>Services</b> and <b>GetRequiredService&lt;T&gt;()</b>: the user's DI scope (the circuit on Blazor Server, the app on WebAssembly) - where an API client comes from in a command declared far from any component.</li>
                    <li><b>Navigation</b>: the <code>NavigationManager</code>, for handlers that navigate.</li>
                    <li><b>Palette</b>: the <code>IBitCommandPaletteService</code> itself, for handlers that register, open or run other commands.</li>
                    <li><b>CancellationToken</b>: cancelled when the palette is closed while the handler is still running. What such a handler returns afterwards is ignored, so a late page or prompt never reopens a palette the user has dismissed. Pass it to every call the handler awaits.</li>
                </ul>
                Run "Inspect the context" after typing part of its name to see the members filled in. The second row is listed whatever
                is typed (<code>AlwaysShow</code>), with the lowest priority so it comes last: type something that matches nothing and run it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cContextCommands" />
                <br />
                @if (cContextLines.Count == 0)
                {
                    <BitText>Run "Inspect the context".</BitText>
                }
                else
                {
                    <ul>
                        @foreach (var (member, value) in cContextLines)
                        {
                            <li><code>@member</code>: @value</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Prompts" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example17CsharpCode" Id="example17">
            <div>
                A handler that needs a value answers with <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult.Prompt(title, onSubmit, ...)</code>: the search
                box becomes the text field, with the title above it, <code>Enter</code> submits and <code>Esc</code> goes back. <code>onSubmit</code>
                runs with the text in <code>ctx.PromptValue</code> and returns the next result like any handler - a toast, another page,
                another prompt.<br />
                <code>initialValue</code> pre-fills the field (the current name, for a rename) and <code>placeholder</code> says what is
                expected while it is empty.<br />
                A prompt can validate what is typed: <code>validate</code> returns null for a value that may be submitted and the message to
                show otherwise. <code>Enter</code> on an invalid value shows and announces the message and marks the box
                <code>aria-invalid</code>, and from then on the message follows the text as it is corrected. A check that needs a server
                belongs in <code>onSubmit</code>, which answers with <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult.Error</code> to keep the prompt open
                for a correction - an expected failure, so a result rather than an exception. Guards (see below) are asked again when a
                prompt is submitted, since what they read may have changed while the value was typed.<br />
                Try the three rows: a plain prompt, one pre-filled with the project's name, and an invitation whose address is validated as
                it is typed and refused by the "server" for ada@@example.com, who is already a member.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cPromptCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitText>Project: <b>@cProjectName</b> · @cPromptStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Multi-select" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example18CsharpCode" Id="example18">
            <div>
                <code>MultiSelect()</code> turns a command's page into a list of checkboxes. <code>Enter</code>, a click or <code>Space</code>
                (on an empty search box) checks or unchecks a row, and <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> (<code>Cmd+Enter</code> on a Mac) runs the
                command's handler with the checked commands in <code>ctx.SelectedCommands</code> - checking a box runs nothing, the submit is
                what acts on the rows. <code>ChildrenPlaceholder</code> is the place to say so in the search box. Give the rows stable
                <code>Id</code>s: the checked state is kept by id.<br />
                A page a handler returns works the same way: <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult.Page(title, commands, placeholder,
                multiSelect: true, onSubmit: ...)</code> runs <code>onSubmit</code> with the checked rows. That is how a chain of steps is
                written - "Create webhook…" asks for an https address first, validated as it is typed, then answers with a page of events,
                and refuses a submit with no event checked with an <code>Error</code>.<br />
                A multi-select page can also search as the user types, with <code>.Source(name, async (query, cancellationToken) =&gt; ...)</code>
                instead of fixed children (the async sources are covered with the modes): rows checked in one search stay checked across the
                next ones, and <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> hands over every checked row, from every search typed on the page. In "Invite
                people…", type <i>ada</i> and check her, then <i>grace</i> and check her, then apply.<br />
                The rows of a multi-select page are choices rather than commands, so they are not found from the root page and have no
                action panel.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cMultiCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitText>@cMultiStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Long-running commands" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19">
            <div>
                A handler that is still running after 150 ms shows the palette busy: a spinner replaces the search icon, the list is
                <code>aria-busy</code>, the running row carries <code>data-running</code>, and "Working…" (<code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.Running</code>)
                is announced to screen readers. A quick handler never flickers. While it runs, a second <code>Enter</code> is ignored, so
                the command cannot be started twice.<br />
                <code>Esc</code> closes the palette and cancels the handler's <code>ctx.CancellationToken</code> - pass it to every call the
                handler awaits, so the work really stops. What the handler returns after that is dropped, so a late page, prompt or toast never
                reopens a palette the user dismissed; a handler that ends by throwing <code>OperationCanceledException</code> simply stops,
                without being reported as a failure. <code>paletteService.CloseAsync()</code> cancels it the same way.<br />
                When the user leaves the page a handler was started on while it runs - <code>Esc</code> out of a prompt whose submit is still
                working - its answer about pages (<code>Back</code>, <code>Page</code>, <code>Prompt</code>, <code>Reset</code>) is dropped
                rather than applied to the page they are on now; closing, toasts and errors still apply.<br />
                An inline palette never closes, so its handlers always run to the end. This one is registered with the service and runs in the
                modal palette: open it, run "Generate report…" (three seconds of work), and press <code>Esc</code> halfway.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("generate report"))">Find it in the palette</BitButton>
                <br /><br />
                <BitText>@cReportStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Errors" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20">
            <div>
                An exception thrown by a handler - or by the loader of a page's children - raises <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.CommandFailed</code>,
                is logged through the palette's <code>ILogger</code>, and is reported in the palette: a handler's failure as a message in the
                footer, announced assertively, a page's as an error state with a <b>Try again</b> button (see "Recent deployments…" under
                Nested pages).<br />
                The message is the localized, generic <code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.CommandFailed</code> ("Couldn’t run “Sync with server”."),
                so an exception's own text - which can name a server, a table or a user - never reaches the screen by accident. A
                <code>CommandFailed</code> subscriber picks another through the event's <code>Message</code> (for example
                <code>e.Exception.Message</code> for a <code>ValidationException</code>, whose text is written for users), or sets it to
                <code>""</code> to show nothing. Setting <code>Handled = true</code> says the subscriber reported the failure itself: the
                palette then does not log it, and <code>ExecuteAsync</code> does not rethrow it.<br />
                A failure in the palette's own UI - a row chosen with the keyboard or the mouse, a global shortcut - is never rethrown, so it can
                neither end a Blazor Server circuit nor vanish into the browser's console. <code>paletteService.ExecuteAsync(id)</code> rethrows an
                unhandled one to the code that called it; with the palette closed, that caller is the only one who can tell the user.
                Cancellation is not an error: a handler that throws <code>OperationCanceledException</code> simply stops - unless it wraps a
                <code>TimeoutException</code>, which is how <code>HttpClient</code> reports a timeout, and is reported like any other failure.<br />
                The application's own listeners are another matter: a <code>CommandExecuted</code> subscriber, an <code>OnCommandExecuted</code>,
                <code>OnClosed</code> or <code>OnQueryChanged</code> that throws is a bug in the app, not a failed command. The command's result is
                still applied - the palette closes, shows its page or its toast - and the exception then goes where one thrown from a click goes:
                to the nearest <code>ErrorBoundary</code>, or, without one, to the host's unhandled-exception handling, which on Blazor Server ends
                the circuit. Wrap the palette in an <code>ErrorBoundary</code> to keep a throwing listener from taking the circuit down.<br />
                Run both rows below: "Sync with server" throws with a server's name in its text, which is not shown; "Publish release" throws a
                <code>ValidationException</code> whose message this page chose to show. Both commands are also registered with the service, so
                the button runs one from code; clear the checkbox to see <code>ExecuteAsync</code> rethrow.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCheckbox Label="Handled = true (this page reports its own failures)" @bind-Value="cHandleFailures" />
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cErrorCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => CRunFromCodeAsync("c-sync"))">Run "Sync with server" from code</BitButton>
                <br /><br />
                <BitText>@cExecuteStatus</BitText>
                <div class="c-log" aria-label="What the CommandFailed subscriber saw">
                    @if (cFailures.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <div>The CommandFailed subscriber has seen nothing yet.</div>
                    }
                    @foreach (var failure in cFailures)
                    {
                        <div>@failure</div>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Guards" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example21CsharpCode" Id="example21">
            <div>
                <code>VisibleWhen</code> and <code>Disabled</code> decide what is listed; a guard decides, every time a command is about to run,
                whether it may - a permission, a plan, a feature flag. Guards run at execution time, not while the list is built on every
                keystroke: hide what a user can never run with <code>VisibleWhen</code>, and enforce it with a guard.<br />
                A guard is asked on every path a command takes: a row chosen with the keyboard or the mouse, <code>Mod+Enter</code>, a global
                shortcut, <code>paletteService.ExecuteAsync(id)</code> - and again for the second <code>Enter</code> of a confirmation and when a
                prompt or a multi-select page is submitted, since what it reads can change in between. <code>OpenAsync(pageCommandId: ...)</code>
                asks a page's guards before the page is listed or loaded. The guard sees a <code>BitCommandPaletteGuardContext</code>: the
                <code>Command</code> about to run, the <code>Trigger</code> that started it (<code>Palette</code>, <code>Shortcut</code> or
                <code>Programmatic</code>) and the scope's <code>Services</code>.<br />
                There are three places for one:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Per command</b>: <code>.Guard(ctx =&gt; plan.IsPro, "Exports need the Pro plan.")</code> for a check that needs nothing async, or <code>.Guard(async (ctx, cancellationToken) =&gt; ...)</code> returning a <code>BitCommandPaletteGuardResult</code>. A command has one guard: a later <code>.Guard</code> replaces an earlier one, so combine checks in one.</li>
                    <li><b>Per page</b>: a page's guard covers every command under it - its registered descendants however they run, and, from the palette, whatever its page lists, lazily loaded children and source results included. So does the guard of a command whose handler returned a page, over that page's rows and its multi-select submit, and a row's guard over its secondary actions.</li>
                    <li><b>Registered</b>: for rules that hold for many commands, implement <code>IBitCommandPaletteCommandGuard</code> and register it with <code>builder.Services.AddBitCommandPaletteCommandGuard&lt;TGuard&gt;()</code>. It is resolved from the scope, so it can use scoped services such as an <code>AuthenticationStateProvider</code>, and reads the command's <code>Tags</code> or <code>Data</code> for a convention of your own. A guard registered through DI is part of the app's startup, so this page cannot add one at runtime: its code is in the sample below.</li>
                </ul>
                The order is the command's ancestors' guards (outermost first), its own, then the registered ones, and the first refusal wins.
                Submitting a multi-select page also asks about every row checked. A command whose ancestor the palette cannot find (its
                <code>Parent</code> not registered, out of reach with <code>IncludeRegisteredCommands</code> off, or nested deeper than 32 levels)
                is refused, since that ancestor's guard could not be asked.<br />
                <code>BitCommandPaletteGuardResult.Allow</code> lets it run, <code>Deny(message)</code> refuses it, and a <code>bool</code> converts
                to either (<code>default</code> refuses, so a guard that forgets to answer fails closed). Read <code>IsAllowed</code> rather than
                comparing results: two refusals with different messages are different values.<br />
                A refused command does not run, arm or open: the footer shows the guard's message, or <code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.CommandRefused</code>
                ("You can’t run “Export as PDF”.") when it gave none (<code>Deny("")</code> shows nothing), <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.CommandRefused</code>
                is raised with the command, the trigger and the message, and an information entry is logged. <code>ExecuteAsync</code> does not
                throw for a refusal - the event is how code, a shortcut or an audit log hears of it while the palette is closed. A guard that
                throws refuses and is logged as an error. A guard still deciding about a row shows the palette busy after 150 ms and turns a
                second <code>Enter</code> away; closing the palette gives up on it. Without any guard the check costs nothing.<br />
                Guards keep the palette honest; they are not the last line. On WebAssembly they run in the browser with the rest of the app, so
                the server still checks every action a handler asks it to perform.<br />
                Below, "Export as PDF" needs the Pro plan and "Admin" - a page whose guard takes a moment, like a server call - needs an admin.
                Try them from the list, with <code>G</code> then <code>X</code> anywhere on the page, and from code, with and without the boxes checked.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCheckbox Label="Pro plan" @bind-Value="cIsPro" />
                    <BitCheckbox Label="Admin" @bind-Value="cIsAdmin" />
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cGuardCommands" />
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.ExecuteAsync("c-guard-export"))">Run "Export as PDF" from code</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync(pageCommandId: "c-guard-admin"))">Open the Admin page</BitButton>
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitText>@cGuardStatus</BitText>
                <div class="c-log" aria-label="What the CommandRefused subscriber saw">
                    @if (cRefusals.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <div>Nothing refused yet.</div>
                    }
                    @foreach (var refusal in cRefusals)
                    {
                        <div>@refusal</div>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Nested pages" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
            <div>
                A command with children opens a page listing them, with breadcrumbs and a back button above the search box. <code>Backspace</code>
                or <code>←</code> on an empty search box, the back button and <code>Esc</code> go back one page, and the query typed before the page
                opened comes back with it; with text in the box, <code>Backspace</code> just deletes a character. Each ancestor in the breadcrumbs
                is a button back to its page. There are four ways to give a command a page:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Static children</b>: <code>.Children(a, b, c)</code> (or <code>BitCommandPaletteCmd.Page(title, a, b, c)</code>). Children registered with the service are flattened into the registry with their <code>Parent</code> set, so <code>Find</code>, <code>ExecuteAsync</code>, shortcuts and <code>OpenAsync(pageCommandId: ...)</code> reach them like any other command. "Language" below answers <code>Back</code> from each option, to land on the list again.</li>
                    <li><b>Lazily loaded children</b>: <code>.Children(async (ctx, cancellationToken) =&gt; ..., placeholder)</code>. The page opens at once with a loading state and fills in when the provider returns; the provider runs each time the page opens, with a context whose <code>Services</code> come from the user's scope - where a real app's API client comes from. The token is cancelled when the page is left, the palette is closed and reset, or the page is loaded again, and an abandoned load changes nothing. A provider that throws gets an error state with a <b>Try again</b> button (<code>Enter</code> retries too) and raises <code>CommandFailed</code>: "Recent deployments…" fails every other time.</li>
                    <li><b>Declarative children</b>: nested <code>BitCommandPaletteOption</code>s in markup, as "Theme" is declared below.</li>
                    <li><b>Pages a handler returns</b>: <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandResult.Page(...)</code>, for a list that depends on what the handler found out when it ran ("Switch branch…"). These commands are not registered anywhere: they live on the page only. A page can also search as the user types, with <code>.Source(...)</code>, as "Invite people…" in Multi-select does.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Finding nested commands from the root.</b> Nested commands are found from the root page too (<code>SearchNestedCommands</code>,
                on by default, as in kbar): typing <i>dark</i> lists <b>Theme › Dark</b> under Theme's group, and <i>theme dark</i> matches the
                ancestors' titles as well (<code>Weights.Path</code>). A root command and a nested one that match equally well are listed root
                first. Choosing a nested command found this way opens with its ancestors' pages behind it, so <code>Esc</code> goes up one level at
                a time, and recently used and pinned nested commands are listed on the root page with their path. Only commands registered with
                the service or declared in markup are found - not the children of commands passed to an inline palette's <code>Commands</code>,
                and not a lazily loaded page's, which do not exist until it opens - and only when every ancestor is visible and enabled and none is a
                multi-select page. Modes never include them. Turn it off with <code>SearchNestedCommands="false"</code> on the palette or in the options.<br />
                <b>Opening directly on a page.</b> <code>paletteService.OpenAsync(pageCommandId: "...")</code> opens the modal palette straight
                inside a registered command's page, with its ancestors' pages behind it, so the breadcrumbs show the whole path; a
                <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code> takes the same <code>PageCommandId</code>, so a "Change theme" button can be a trigger that
                lands on the theme options rather than on the root list. The page's guards are asked first. The buttons below open the modal
                palette on "Preferences › Editor theme", registered with the service.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="cNestedCommands">
                    <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Theme" Value="@cTheme" ChildrenPlaceholder="Pick a theme…">
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Light" Checked="@(cTheme == "Light")" OnExecute="@(() => CSetTheme("Light"))" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="Dark" Checked="@(cTheme == "Dark")" OnExecute="@(() => CSetTheme("Dark"))" />
                        <BitCommandPaletteOption Title="High contrast" Checked="@(cTheme == "High contrast")" OnExecute="@(() => CSetTheme("High contrast"))" />
                    </BitCommandPaletteOption>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <BitText>@cNestedStatus</BitText>
                <br />
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync(pageCommandId: "c-editor-theme"))">Open on Editor theme</BitButton>
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Change editor theme" PageCommandId="c-editor-theme" ShowShortcut="false" />
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("solarized"))">Search "solarized" from the root</BitButton>
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitText>Editor theme: <b>@cEditorTheme</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Recents, pins and the action panel" RazorCode="@example23RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example23CsharpCode" Id="example23">
            <div>
                On the empty query of the root page, pinned commands are listed first, in the order they were pinned, and the most recently used
                ones next - up to <code>MaxRecents</code> (5) of the options. Every run is recorded unless the command has
                <code>ExcludeFromHistory()</code> (a to-do, a search result, anything that will not be there next time), and the counts also feed
                frecency - how often and how lately a command was used - which lifts familiar commands when a query is typed
                (<code>FrecencyWeight</code> in the options).<br />
                <code>Tab</code> - or <code>→</code> on an empty search box, or a right click - opens the action panel of the active row: its own
                <code>.Actions(...)</code> first, then <b>Pin to top</b> or <b>Unpin</b>, <b>Remove from recents</b> when it has been used, and
                <b>Clear recent commands</b>, which forgets every use and keeps the pins - a privacy control within reach of the list it clears.
                Some keys skip the panel: <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> (<code>Cmd+Enter</code>) runs the row's first action directly - on a link
                without actions it opens the link in a new tab, as GitHub's palette does - <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> (<code>Cmd+Shift+P</code>)
                pins or unpins, and <code>Shift+Delete</code> forgets. A row with no actions and <code>ExcludeFromHistory</code> has no panel.<br />
                History is command ids, counts and times - never queries. It lives in <code>BitCommandPaletteHistory</code>, a scoped service an
                app can inject too: <code>Pinned()</code>, <code>Recents(max)</code>, <code>Get(id)</code>, <code>IsPinned(id)</code>,
                <code>Frecency(id)</code>, <code>TogglePinAsync</code>, <code>RemoveAsync</code>, <code>ClearAsync</code> and a
                <code>Changed</code> event (raised off the renderer's thread, so re-render through <code>InvokeAsync</code>). The default store keeps it
                in the browser's <code>localStorage</code> under <code>HistoryStorageKey</code> ("bit-command-palette.history"), capped at
                <code>MaxHistoryEntries</code> (200, pins never pruned) and validated when it is read back. To keep it elsewhere - per user on the
                server, or nowhere - register a store at startup (see the sample). Turn history off for every palette with
                <code>options.EnableHistory = false</code>, or on one palette with <code>EnableHistory="false"</code>: no recents, no pins, no
                frecency and no action panel for rows without actions.<br />
                Run a few rows below, press <code>Tab</code> on one, pin another with <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code>, clear the search box, and watch
                the lists. The people rows add two actions of their own; "Go to the Button demo" is a link, for <code>Ctrl+Enter</code>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCheckbox Label="EnableHistory" @bind-Value="cEnableHistory" />
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="cEnableHistory" Commands="cHistoryCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitText>@cHistoryStatus</BitText>
                <BitText>Pinned: <b>@CHistoryText(cHistory.Pinned(), "nothing - press Tab on a row and pick Pin to top")</b></BitText>
                <BitText>Recent: <b>@CHistoryText(cHistory.Recents(int.MaxValue), "nothing used yet")</b></BitText>
                <br />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => cHistory.ClearAsync())">Clear recent commands</BitButton>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Fuzzy matching" RazorCode="@example24RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example24CsharpCode" Id="example24">
            <div>
                <b>BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher</b>, the palette's default matcher, follows fzf's algorithm. A query matches a text when its
                characters appear in it in order, with anything in between; among all such alignments, dynamic programming keeps the one
                with the best score, built from these public constants of the class:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>ScoreMatch</code> (16) for every matched character; <code>ScoreGapStart</code> (-3) for the first character a gap skips and <code>ScoreGapExtension</code> (-1) for each further one.</li>
                    <li><code>BonusBoundary</code> (8) for a match at the start of a word: <code>gs</code> finds <b>G</b>o to <b>s</b>ettings.</li>
                    <li><code>BonusCamel</code> (7) for a camelCase hump or a letter-to-digit step: <code>fm</code> finds <b>F</b>uzzy<b>M</b>atcher.cs.</li>
                    <li><code>BonusNonWord</code> (8) for punctuation the query itself typed, and <code>BonusConsecutive</code> (4) for a character right after the previous match, carrying the bonus that started the run.</li>
                    <li><code>BonusFirstCharMultiplier</code> (2): the first query character's bonus counts double; <code>BonusCaseMatch</code> (1) for a character whose case matches exactly.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Smart case</b>: a term typed all in lower case ignores case, a term with a capital letter respects it - <code>mcp</code> finds
                "MCP server", <code>Mcp</code> does not. <code>new BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher { IgnoreCase = true }</code> turns that off.
                Between equal alignments the shorter text and the earlier match win, and the ranker puts equal ranks in title order.
                <br />
                The positions of the winning alignment are what the palette <b>highlights</b> with a <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> in titles and
                subtitles - never splitting a letter from its combining accents or an emoji from its other half. Turn it off with
                <code>HighlightMatches="false"</code> on one palette or <code>options.HighlightMatches = false</code> for all of them, and render
                the same highlighting in markup of your own with <code>&lt;BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="..." Positions="..." /&gt;</code>,
                as the table here does. While a query is typed, results stay under their group headings (<code>GroupWhenSearching</code>, on by
                default); <code>GroupWhenSearching="false"</code> makes them one flat ranked list.
                <br />
                A field longer than <code>BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher.MaxOptimalLength</code> (4,096 characters), such as a long description, is
                aligned greedily in linear time instead, as fzf does: the term is still found and highlighted, the alignment just is not always
                the best one. A query is searched up to <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.MaxLength</code> (256) characters; a longer one matches no
                command (<code>IsTooLong</code>) but is not cut short: commands with <code>AlwaysShow</code> are still listed, and async sources
                and handlers still receive all of it.
                <br />
                Type in the palette below. The table runs the palette's own steps with the public API on every keystroke -
                <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.Parse</code>, the <code>Match(command, query)</code> extension of
                <code>BitCommandPaletteMatcherExtensions</code> and <code>BitCommandPaletteRanker.Rank</code> - and shows each command's score and
                final rank. <code>EnableHistory="false"</code> keeps your own usage (frecency) out of the palette, so it lists the commands in
                exactly the table's order. Try <code>gs</code>, <code>fm</code>, <code>rc</code>, <code>mcp</code> and <code>Mcp</code>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="dLabCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Try gs, fm, rc, mcp or Mcp…"
                                   OnQueryChanged="DLabQueryChanged" />
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>#</th><th>Command</th><th class="d-num">Score</th><th class="d-num">Rank</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @if (dLabMatches.Count == 0)
                            {
                                <tr><td colspan="4">No command matches "@dLabQuery" as typed.</td></tr>
                            }
                            @foreach (var (match, index) in dLabMatches.Select((m, i) => (m, i)))
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@(index + 1)</td>
                                    <td><BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@match.Command.Title" Positions="@match.TitlePositions" /></td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(match.Score)</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(match.Rank)</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Text as people type it" RazorCode="@example25RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example25CsharpCode" Id="example25">
            <div>
                People type what their keyboard gives them, not what the data stores. <code>BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher.Normalize</code> (on
                by default) folds both sides before comparing them:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Latin letters lose their diacritics</b>: <code>cafe</code> finds "Café", <code>lodz</code> finds "Łódź" (letters with a stroke too, as in fzf) and <code>turkce</code> finds "Türkçe". A term that types a diacritic itself means that letter, as in fzf: <code>café</code> is matched as written.</li>
                    <li><b>Arabic and Persian letters that differ only by the keyboard they were typed on are one letter</b>: the forms of yeh (<code>ي ى ئ</code> and <code>ی</code>) and kaf (<code>ك</code> and <code>ک</code>), alef with hamza or madda (<code>أ إ آ ٱ</code> and <code>ا</code>), teh marbuta and heh with yeh (<code>ة ۀ</code> and <code>ه</code>), and waw with hamza (<code>ؤ</code> and <code>و</code>). A name stored from an Arabic keyboard is found from a Persian one, and the other way round.</li>
                    <li><b>Digits are digits</b>: Arabic-Indic and Persian digits are ASCII digits - <code>۱۲</code> finds "Order 12", <code>٣</code> finds "Step 3" - and fullwidth ASCII, which some input methods type, is ASCII.</li>
                </ul>
                The folding is done by <code>BitCommandPaletteTextFold</code>, an internal class shared by the fuzzy matcher,
                <code>BitCommandPaletteSubstringMatcher</code> and the query operators, so <code>'exact</code>, <code>^prefix</code>,
                <code>suffix$</code> and <code>!negate</code> fold exactly the same way. It is not public API; what an app controls is the
                matcher's <code>Normalize</code> property. Every mapping is one character to one, so a position in the folded text is the same
                position in the stored one and the highlight lands on the letters as they are stored: typing <code>cafe</code> marks all of
                "Café", the é included, and nothing about the stored data changes.
                <br />
                <code>new BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher { Normalize = false }</code> matches every character literally, like fzf's
                <code>--literal</code>; set it as <code>options.Matcher</code> to make every palette of the app literal (see "A custom matcher").
                The palette below always uses the app's matcher - type the examples into it. The table matches each example against the same
                commands with the default matcher, or, with the switch on, with a literal one, so you can see what the folding finds.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="dFoldCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Try cafe, lodz, علی, کتاب or ۱۲…" />
                <br />
                <BitToggle Label="Match literally (Normalize = false)" @bind-Value="dFoldLiteral" />
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Typed</th><th>Finds</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var example in dFoldExamples)
                            {
                                var match = DFoldMatch(example.Typed);
                                <tr>
                                    <td dir="auto"><code>@example.Typed</code></td>
                                    <td dir="auto">
                                        @if (match is null)
                                        {
                                            <i>nothing</i>
                                        }
                                        else
                                        {
                                            <BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@match.Command.Title" Positions="@match.TitlePositions" />
                                        }
                                    </td>
                                    <td>@example.Why</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Query operators" RazorCode="@example26RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example26CsharpCode" Id="example26">
            <div>
                A query is split on spaces into terms, and <b>every term has to match</b> (AND), each against whichever field of the command
                scores it best, in any order. A term is fuzzy unless it carries one of fzf's operators:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>'export</code> - <b>exact</b>: the text itself, with no gaps, anywhere in a field.</li>
                    <li><code>^set</code> - <b>prefix</b>: a field starts with it.</li>
                    <li><code>project$</code> - <b>suffix</b>: a field ends with it.</li>
                    <li><code>!minimap</code> - <b>negation</b>: drops every command whose title, subtitle, group or keywords contain the text (description and tags are not looked at). It adds nothing to the score.</li>
                    <li><code>"word wrap"</code> - a <b>quoted phrase</b>: one exact term, spaces included. <code>!"..."</code> excludes a phrase, <code>^"..."</code> and <code>"..."$</code> anchor it. Curly quotes, which some keyboards type, work as well as straight ones, and a quote left open takes the rest of the query while it is being typed, so typing one never shows "no results" midway.</li>
                    <li><code>^change | ^set</code> - <b>alternatives</b>: a <code>|</code> standing on its own between two terms means either will do, as in fzf. <code>!docker | git</code> holds for anything about git, or not about docker.</li>
                </ul>
                The operators follow the same folding rules as the matcher (<code>'cafe</code> finds "Café"), and they work with any matcher,
                the one you plug in included. <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.Parse(text)</code> is the palette's own parser and is public: its
                <code>Terms</code> give each term's <code>Text</code>, its <code>Operator</code> (a <code>BitCommandPaletteQueryOperator</code>:
                <code>Fuzzy</code>, <code>Exact</code>, <code>Prefix</code>, <code>Suffix</code> or <code>Negate</code>) and its <code>Group</code>
                - terms sharing a group are alternatives of which one has to hold. The palette parses a query once per keystroke, not once per
                command.
                <br />
                Type the examples of the table into the palette; the line under it shows, live, what <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.Parse</code>
                made of what you typed (the palette reports its query through <code>OnQueryChanged</code>).
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="dOperatorCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Try ^set, project$ or show !minimap…"
                                   OnQueryChanged="DOperatorQueryChanged" />
                <div class="d-terms">
                    <span>BitCommandPaletteQuery.Parse:</span>
                    @if (dOperatorQuery.IsTooLong)
                    {
                        <span>longer than @BitCommandPaletteQuery.MaxLength characters - no command matches.</span>
                    }
                    else if (dOperatorQuery.Terms.Count == 0)
                    {
                        <span>no terms - every command matches.</span>
                    }
                    @for (var i = 0; i < dOperatorQuery.Terms.Count; i++)
                    {
                        var term = dOperatorQuery.Terms[i];
                        if (i > 0)
                        {
                            <span>@(dOperatorQuery.Terms[i - 1].Group == term.Group ? "or" : "and")</span>
                        }
                        <span class="d-term">@term.Operator <code>@term.Text</code></span>
                    }
                </div>
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Query</th><th>Means</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var example in dOperatorExamples)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>@example.Query</code></td>
                                    <td>@example.Means</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Typos" RazorCode="@example27RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example27CsharpCode" Id="example27">
            <div>
                A query that matches nothing is tried once more <b>allowing a typo per term</b>, so <code>settigns</code> still finds
                "Settings". What counts as one typo is one edit of the optimal string alignment: a <b>missing</b>, <b>extra</b> or <b>wrong</b>
                letter, or two neighbouring letters <b>swapped</b>. A term of 4 to 7 characters may have one, a term of 8 to 64 characters two;
                shorter terms (one edit would make them match almost anything), longer ones and terms with an operator get no second chance. A
                missing letter is usually found without the retry, since the rest of the term is still in order and the fuzzy matcher allows
                gaps.
                <br />
                Only a query that found nothing is retried, so a query that works never gets noisier. The close matches are scored below every
                exact one, async sources that opt into the matcher (<code>ApplyMatcher</code>) are re-scored the same way, and the list is
                marked and announced "No exact matches. Showing close ones." (<code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.ApproximateResults</code>);
                <code>BitCommandPaletteMatch.IsApproximate</code> tells an <code>ItemTemplate</code> a row is a close match. A field is read a
                machine word at a time, so a typo is found in a long description too.
                <br />
                The retry belongs to the palette, not to the matcher: calling a matcher directly, as the tables of the previous sections do,
                never finds a typo. Turn it off for every palette with <code>options.TypoTolerance = false</code>, or for one palette with
                <code>TypoTolerance="false"</code> - flip the switch and try <code>settigns</code>, <code>langauge</code>
                (swapped), <code>privacyy</code> (extra), <code>notefications</code> (wrong) and <code>apearence</code> (two).
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitToggle Label="TypoTolerance" @bind-Value="dTypoTolerance" />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="dTypoCommands"
                                   TypoTolerance="dTypoTolerance"
                                   Placeholder="Try settigns, langauge, privacyy or notefications…" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Field weights" RazorCode="@example28RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example28CsharpCode" Id="example28">
            <div>
                Each term of a query is scored against every searchable field of a command, and the field with the best <b>weighted</b> score
                counts - so a hit in the title beats the same hit in a keyword, which beats one in the subtitle. The weights are a
                <code>BitCommandPaletteMatchWeights</code>, set for the whole app through <code>options.Weights</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Title</code> 1.0</li>
                    <li><code>Keyword</code> 0.9 - <b>Keywords</b> are the synonyms a command is also found by: <code>.Keywords("logo", "color")</code> in C#, <code>Keywords="logo, color"</code> on a <code>BitCommandPaletteOption</code>. They are not shown, except that a row without a subtitle shows the keyword that matched, so the user sees why it is listed (<code>BitCommandPaletteMatch.MatchedKeyword</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>Subtitle</code> 0.7</li>
                    <li><code>Group</code> 0.5</li>
                    <li><code>Path</code> 0.5 - the titles of a nested command's ancestors when it is found from the root page: <code>theme dark</code> finds "Dark" on the page of "Theme".</li>
                    <li><code>Description</code> 0.4</li>
                    <li><code>Tag</code> 0.3</li>
                </ul>
                With several terms, each one picks its own best field and their scores add up. Only the title's and the subtitle's matched
                characters are highlighted. To let descriptions count for more, for example:
                <code>builder.Services.AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices(commandPalette: options =&gt; options.Weights.Description = 0.6)</code>.
                <br />
                Every command below holds "color" in a different field. Type <code>color</code> (or <code>col</code>, <code>clr</code>) into
                the palette: it lists them in the order of the weights. The table shows, for what you typed taken as one fuzzy term, the field
                that won, its raw score, its weight and the product. <code>GroupWhenSearching="false"</code> keeps the palette one flat ranked
                list, so the group of "Contrast checker" does not move it under a heading of its own.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   GroupWhenSearching="false"
                                   Commands="dWeightCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Try color, col or clr…"
                                   OnQueryChanged="DWeightQueryChanged" />
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Field that won</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Raw score</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Weight</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Weighted</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var row in DWeightRows())
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@row.Command</td>
                                    <td>@row.Field</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(row.Raw)</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(row.Weight)</td>
                                    <td class="d-num"><b>@DFormat(row.Raw * row.Weight)</b></td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Priority and frecency" RazorCode="@example29RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example29CsharpCode" Id="example29">
            <div>
                With a query, <b>the match decides and the rest only nudges it</b>:
                <code>rank = score + Priority × PriorityWeight + ln(1 + frecency) × FrecencyWeight</code>, with
                <code>options.PriorityWeight</code> 0.25 and <code>options.FrecencyWeight</code> 6 by default - a clearly better match always
                wins, and among close ones the command you use wins. Equal ranks go by title. On the empty query there is nothing to match, so
                <b>priority leads and frecency orders what is left</b> (<code>priority × 1000 + frecency</code>), inside each group, and
                commands of equal priority keep the order they were registered or declared in. Only the best <code>options.MaxResults</code>
                (150) rows are kept per query; pinned and recent commands are listed beyond it.
                <br />
                A priority is any <code>int</code>; <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandPriority</code> names the usual levels: <code>Lowest</code>
                (-100, a feedback row with <code>AlwaysShow</code>), <code>Low</code> (-50, documentation hits), <code>Normal</code> (0, the
                default), <code>High</code> (50) and <code>Highest</code> (100). Set it with <code>.Priority(...)</code> in C#, with
                <code>Priority</code> on a <code>BitCommandPaletteOption</code>, or on a <code>BitCommandPaletteGroup</code> for all of its
                options that set none.
                <br />
                <b>Frecency</b> is Firefox's idea: the number of uses, capped at 20, times a weight for how long ago the last one was - 100
                within 4 hours, 70 within a day, 50 within three days, 30 within a week, 10 within a month, 5 after that - divided by 10. The
                palette records every run in its history (see recents), and <code>BitCommandPaletteHistory.ComputeFrecency(usage, now)</code>
                is the formula, public.
                <br />
                The table ranks five commands with a <code>BitCommandPaletteRanker</code> built over options with the sliders' weights, using
                frecencies computed from made-up usage: "Report a bug" was used 3 times five days ago, and "Weekly report" as often as the
                third slider says, two hours ago. Raise its uses and watch it overtake the others on <code>report</code>; raise
                <code>PriorityWeight</code> and "Reports overview" (High) pulls ahead; clear the query and priority alone decides. The palette
                lists the same commands with the app's weights and no history (<code>EnableHistory="false"</code>).
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="d-toolbar">
                    <BitTextField Label="Query" @bind-Value="dRankQuery" Immediate />
                    <BitSlider Label="PriorityWeight" Min="0" Max="2" Step="0.05" @bind-Value="dPriorityWeight" />
                    <BitSlider Label="FrecencyWeight" Min="0" Max="20" Step="1" @bind-Value="dFrecencyWeight" />
                    <BitSlider Label="Uses of Weekly report" Min="0" Max="20" Step="1" @bind-Value="dRankWeeklyUses" />
                </div>
                <BitText>rank = score + priority × @DFormat(dPriorityWeight) + ln(1 + frecency) × @DFormat(dFrecencyWeight)</BitText>
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>#</th>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Priority</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Frecency</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Score</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Rank</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var (match, index) in DRankRows().Select((m, i) => (m, i)))
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@(index + 1)</td>
                                    <td><BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@match.Command.Title" Positions="@match.TitlePositions" /></td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@match.Command.Priority</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(match.Frecency)</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(match.Score)</td>
                                    <td class="d-num"><b>@DFormat(match.Rank)</b></td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="dRankCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Type report…" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="A custom matcher" RazorCode="@example30RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example30CsharpCode" Id="example30">
            <div>
                The matcher is pluggable. <code>IBitCommandPaletteMatcher</code> is one method - <code>Match(query, text)</code> - returning a
                <code>BitCommandPaletteFieldMatch(Matched, Score, Positions)</code>: whether the text matches, how well, and the indexes of the
                characters to highlight (<code>BitCommandPaletteFieldMatch.None</code> for no match, <code>Empty</code> for an empty query). The
                palette calls it once per fuzzy term per field, so multi-term queries, the operators (which fold diacritics and Arabic-script
                letters with any matcher but a literal one), field weights, ranking and the typo retry all keep working with a matcher of your
                own. Set it for the whole app with <code>options.Matcher</code> in the <code>commandPalette</code> argument of <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</code>; the ready-made ones
                are:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher.Default</code> - the default; <code>new BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher { IgnoreCase = true }</code> drops smart case, <code>{ Normalize = false }</code> matches literally.</li>
                    <li><code>BitCommandPaletteSubstringMatcher.Default</code> - a case-insensitive substring matcher that scores a match at the start higher, for data where fuzziness hurts more than it helps (codes, part numbers). It folds like the fuzzy one unless its <code>Normalize</code> is false.</li>
                </ul>
                Matching can also be called directly - in a test, or in a tool that explains a search: the <code>Match(command, query, weights)</code>
                extension of <code>BitCommandPaletteMatcherExtensions</code> matches a whole command (with a query string, or one parsed once with
                <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.Parse</code> for many commands) and returns a <code>BitCommandPaletteMatch</code>, or null. It carries
                the <code>Score</code>, the <code>TitlePositions</code> and <code>SubtitlePositions</code> to highlight, the
                <code>MatchedKeyword</code>, the <code>Path</code> of a nested command found from the root, <code>IsApproximate</code> for a typo
                match, <code>FromSource</code> and <code>SourceName</code> for a source's result, and - once <code>BitCommandPaletteRanker.Rank</code>
                (injectable, or created over options) has ordered the matches as the palette does - its <code>Rank</code> and
                <code>Frecency</code>. Matching runs over the command's own fields; an async source opts into it with <code>ApplyMatcher</code>.
                <br />
                <code>options.Matcher</code> is one setting for the whole app, so instead of changing it, this demo runs each matcher directly
                over the commands of the "Fuzzy matching" section. <code>DPrefixMatcher</code> is a complete custom matcher: a field matches when
                it starts with the term. Compare <code>re</code>, <code>rc</code> and <code>Mcp</code> across them.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitChoiceGroup Label="Matcher" Horizontal TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string" @bind-Value="dMatcherName">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Fuzzy, smart case" Value="@("fuzzy")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Fuzzy, ignore case" Value="@("ignore-case")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Fuzzy, literal" Value="@("literal")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Substring" Value="@("substring")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="DPrefixMatcher" Value="@("prefix")" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <br />
                <BitTextField Label="Query" @bind-Value="dMatcherQuery" Immediate />
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>#</th><th>Command</th><th class="d-num">Score</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var (match, index) in DMatcherRows().Select((m, i) => (m, i)))
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@(index + 1)</td>
                                    <td><BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@match.Command.Title" Positions="@match.TitlePositions" /></td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@DFormat(match.Score)</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Modes" RazorCode="@example31RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example31CsharpCode" Id="example31">
            <div>
                A <b>mode</b> is a prefix typed at the start of the query - <code>&gt;</code> in VS Code, <code>#</code> and <code>@@</code> on
                GitHub - that scopes the palette to part of the registry, to its own async sources, or both. A
                <code>BitCommandPaletteCommandMode</code> has:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Prefix</code> (required) - what activates it. The longest matching prefix wins, so <code>&gt;&gt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> can coexist.</li>
                    <li><code>Name</code> (required) - the chip shown next to the search box while the mode is active, and the title of its hint.</li>
                    <li><code>Description</code> - the hint's subtitle.</li>
                    <li><code>Placeholder</code> - the search box's placeholder while the mode is active.</li>
                    <li><code>Tags</code> - only commands carrying one of these tags (<code>.Tags(...)</code> in C#, <code>Tags</code> on an option or a group).</li>
                    <li><code>Groups</code> - only commands in one of these groups.</li>
                    <li><code>HideRegistryCommands</code> - no registry commands at all, only the mode's sources.</li>
                    <li><code>Sources</code> - the async sources asked in this mode, instead of the default mode's (see "Async sources").</li>
                    <li><code>ShowHint</code> (default true) - whether the mode is listed as a hint.</li>
                </ul>
                Register modes for the whole app in <code>options.Modes</code> in the <code>commandPalette</code> argument of <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</code>, or at runtime with
                <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.RegisterMode(mode)</code>, which returns an <code>IDisposable</code> that removes the mode again:
                this page registers its modes when it opens and disposes them when you leave. <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.Modes</code> lists
                what is registered - the table below. On the empty query of the default mode the palette lists every mode with
                <code>ShowHint</code> under a "Modes" heading, so they can be discovered without reading any docs, and picking a hint types its
                prefix; <code>options.ShowModeHints = false</code> drops the whole group. A mode scopes the registry and its sources only:
                nested commands are never found from a mode.
                <br />
                To open the modal palette directly in a mode, give a <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code> a <code>Query</code> that starts with
                the prefix (<code>Query="&gt;"</code>), or call <code>paletteService.OpenAsync("&gt;")</code>. Modes are registered with the
                service, so a palette has them only while <code>IncludeRegisteredCommands</code> is on: the inline palette below leaves it on and
                lists every command registered on this page, with the mode hints at the top. Type <code>&gt;</code> for the commands tagged
                "command", <code>?</code> for the Help group, or <code>&gt;&gt;</code> - a mode with no hint - for everything.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Query=">" Label="Run a command" ShowShortcut="false" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Query="?" Label="Help" ShowShortcut="false" />
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline Placeholder="Type >, ? or >>, or pick a mode…" />
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Mode</th><th>Lists</th><th>Hint</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var mode in paletteService.Modes)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>@mode.Prefix</code></td>
                                    <td><b>@mode.Name</b> @mode.Description</td>
                                    <td>@DModeScope(mode)</td>
                                    <td>@(mode.ShowHint ? "Yes" : "No")</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Async sources" RazorCode="@example32RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example32CsharpCode" Id="example32">
            <div>
                An <b>async source</b> (<code>IBitCommandPaletteSource</code>) searches what the registry does not hold - an API, a database, a
                search index - as the user types. It has a <code>Name</code> (the heading of its results, and of its retry row) and
                <code>SearchAsync(query, context, cancellationToken)</code>, and three members with defaults: <code>MinQueryLength</code>
                (1; 0 also asks on the empty query, so a mode opens on a list), <code>MaxResults</code> (20, the most rows taken from it) and
                <code>ApplyMatcher</code> (false). Give every result an <code>Id</code> of its own - two people can share a name, and recents and
                pins go by the id. The <code>BitCommandPaletteSourceContext</code> says which mode asked (<code>ModePrefix</code>), which page
                asked (<code>ParentCommandId</code>) and carries the user's <code>Services</code>, to resolve a scoped API client from.
                <br />
                <b>Re-ranked or ordered.</b> With <code>ApplyMatcher = true</code> the palette scores what the source returns with its own matcher
                and sorts it in with everything else: the source only has to return candidates, and its rows are highlighted like the rest.
                With <code>false</code>, the source's own order is kept and its rows are listed as one block under its name - right for a server
                that already sorts by relevance.
                <br />
                <b>Debounced, cancelled, timed out.</b> A source is asked only after <code>options.DebounceMs</code> (150 ms) without typing, so
                most keystrokes never reach it, and a search still running when the next one starts is cancelled through its token - hand the
                token to whatever waits, and stale results never show. While any source is pending a spinner replaces the search icon, and a mode
                or page with nothing to show yet shows "Searching…" (<code>LoadingTemplate</code> replaces it). A source that throws, or has not
                answered after <code>options.SourceTimeoutMs</code> (10 s; 0 waits forever) - whether or not it honours its token - is logged and
                leaves a row in its place, "Couldn’t search Service status" in the danger color (<code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.SourceFailed</code>),
                that asks it again when chosen. The other sources' results stay: one failing API does not take the whole list with it.
                <br />
                <b>Registering them.</b> Sources in <code>options.Sources</code> are asked in the default mode, and so are the ones registered at
                runtime with <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.RegisterSource</code> (which returns a handle that removes it) and the ones resolved
                from DI with <code>services.AddBitCommandPaletteSource&lt;TSource&gt;()</code> - those are scoped, belong to the scope, and stay
                registered whichever palette comes and goes. A mode asks its own <code>Sources</code> instead, and a command's page can ask one
                too (see "Searching from a page"). Two ready-made sources cover the common cases: <code>BitCommandPaletteDelegateSource</code>
                wraps a delegate (taking the query, or the query and the context, or - see "Paging" - a cursor too), and
                <code>BitCommandPaletteStaticSource</code> serves an in-memory list, fixed or read again on every search, through the palette's
                own matcher and weights, returning its best <code>maxResults</code> (50) - always re-ranked, asked from the first character.
                <br />
                This page registered an <code>@@</code> People mode whose sources are a <code>DPeopleSource</code> (re-ranked, 250 ms of fake
                latency) and a <code>BitCommandPaletteStaticSource</code> of teams, and a default-mode "Service status" source - a delegate
                source, ordered, asked from 3 characters - whose behaviour you pick. Open the modal palette and type at your normal speed: the
                table lists every call that reached a source, newest first, and how it ended. Pick "Never answers" and search "status": the
                call is cancelled after 10 s and the retry row appears.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("@"))">Open in People mode</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("@ada lovelace"))">Search @@ada lovelace</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("status"))">Search "status"</BitButton>
                </div>
                <br />
                <BitChoiceGroup Label="The Service status source" Horizontal TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string" @bind-Value="dStatusBehaviour">
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Answers" Value="@("answers")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Throws" Value="@("throws")" />
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Never answers" Value="@("hangs")" />
                </BitChoiceGroup>
                <br />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" OnClick="DClearSourceCalls">Clear the calls</BitButton>
                <div class="d-scroll">
                    <table class="d-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Started</th>
                                <th>Source</th>
                                <th>Query</th>
                                <th>Outcome</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Results</th>
                                <th class="d-num">Took</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @{ var calls = DSourceCallsSnapshot(); }
                            @if (calls.Length == 0)
                            {
                                <tr><td colspan="6">No source has been asked yet.</td></tr>
                            }
                            @foreach (var call in calls)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@call.StartedAt.ToString("HH:mm:ss.fff", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</td>
                                    <td>@call.Source</td>
                                    <td><code>@(call.Query.Length == 0 ? "(empty)" : call.Query)</code></td>
                                    <td>@call.Outcome</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@(call.Outcome == "Completed" ? call.Results.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) : "-")</td>
                                    <td class="d-num">@(call.Outcome == "Running" ? "-" : DMs(call.Took))</td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Paging" RazorCode="@example33RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example33CsharpCode" Id="example33">
            <div>
                A source with more results than it should send at once answers through
                <code>SearchPageAsync(query, cursor, context, cancellationToken)</code>: the first page with a null <code>cursor</code>, each next
                one with the <code>NextCursor</code> of the page before. It returns a <code>BitCommandPaletteSourcePage(Commands, NextCursor,
                TotalCount)</code>; <code>HasMore</code> is true while there is a next cursor. A page with a cursor gets a row after the source's
                results - "Show 50 more…" when the page says <code>TotalCount</code> (<code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.MoreResults</code>), "Show
                more…" when it does not (<code>LoadMore</code>) - which loads the next page, appends it without repeating what is already
                listed, and selects its first row. A next page that fails keeps the pages already listed, with a row to try again. A source that
                implements only <code>SearchAsync</code> answers a single page: <code>SearchPageAsync</code> has a default implementation that
                calls it.
                <br />
                <code>BitCommandPaletteDelegateSource</code> has a paging constructor whose delegate takes the query, the cursor, the context and
                the token. The cursor is whatever the server understands - an offset, as here, or an opaque continuation token.
                <br />
                This page registered a <code>$</code> Packages mode over 60 packages, 10 at a time, with
                <code>MinQueryLength = 0</code> so it opens on a list. Open it, scroll to "Show 50 more…" and choose it; the call log of the
                "Async sources" section shows every page it loaded.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("$"))">Open in Packages mode</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("$acme"))">Search $acme</BitButton>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Searching from a page" RazorCode="@example34RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example34CsharpCode" Id="example34">
            <div>
                A command's page can <b>search as the user types</b> instead of listing what it has - an "Assign to…" over thousands of people,
                which a lazily loaded page would have to download whole. <code>.Source(name, search)</code> on a command builder turns the command
                into a page whose rows come from <code>search(query, cancellationToken)</code>; it is asked when the page opens
                (<code>minQueryLength</code> is 0 by default here, so the page never opens empty) and for every query typed on it, with the same
                debounce, cancellation, timeout, retry row and loading state as the root's sources. <code>.Source(name, searchPage)</code> takes a
                paging delegate - query, cursor, token - with its "Show more…" row, and <code>.Source(IBitCommandPaletteSource)</code> takes any
                source, whose <code>BitCommandPaletteSourceContext.ParentCommandId</code> then says which page asked. <code>ChildrenPlaceholder</code>
                sets the page's placeholder.
                <br />
                On a <code>MultiSelect</code> page the checked rows are kept across searches, so <code>Ctrl+Enter</code> (<code>Cmd+Enter</code>)
                hands the handler everything checked, whichever query listed it.
                <br />
                Open "Assign to…" and search people, open "Request reviews…" and check people from several searches before applying, or open
                "Browse packages…" for a paged page source.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="dPageSourceCommands" />
                <br />
                <BitText>@dAssignStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Performance" RazorCode="@example35RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example35CsharpCode" Id="example35">
            <div>
                A palette searches on every keystroke, so the hot path is written for it, and a registry of thousands of commands stays fast:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>The registry is read once, not per keystroke</b>: the palette keeps a snapshot and takes a new one only when the registry, the commands declared in its markup or its <code>Commands</code> parameter changed. Pass <code>Commands</code> a new collection to change it, or call <code>IBitCommandPaletteService.Refresh()</code> after changing one in place. Global shortcuts are re-evaluated when the snapshot changes, when the palette opens or closes, after a command ran and on <code>Refresh()</code> - not while typing.</li>
                    <li>The query is parsed once per keystroke, not once per command, and the matcher allocates nothing per field but the highlighted positions: it reuses per-thread buffers, keeps one row of scores, rejects texts that do not hold the term before the dynamic programming starts, and aligns a field past <code>MaxOptimalLength</code> greedily without buffers.</li>
                    <li><b>Ranking only sorts what it keeps</b>: only the best <code>options.MaxResults</code> (150) rows are rendered per query, held in a heap and sorted. Raise it for long lists - and turn on <code>Virtualize</code> (on one palette, or <code>options.Virtualize</code>), recommended above about 500 visible rows, which renders only the rows on screen. While it is on, every row is <code>options.RowHeight</code> (44 px) tall, whatever <code>--bit-CommandPalette-row-height</code> says, and each option carries <code>aria-posinset</code> and <code>aria-setsize</code>, since rows cannot be wrapped in their groups.</li>
                    <li>Results arriving from async sources reuse the registry's matches for the same query instead of matching again.</li>
                    <li><b>Typing never lags on WebAssembly</b>, where search and painting share the page's only thread: when a search took longer than a frame (8 ms), the palette lets the keystroke paint first and folds the keys typed meanwhile into one search; navigation keys still act on the latest query. On the server each keystroke round-trips anyway, so it searches right away.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Register, or serve from a source.</b> Registered commands (<code>paletteService.Register(commands)</code>, or a palette's
                <code>Commands</code>) are part of the registry: listed on the empty query, matched by every keystroke, reachable by
                <code>Find</code>, <code>ExecuteAsync</code> and their shortcuts. A <code>BitCommandPaletteStaticSource</code> keeps a large list
                out of the registry instead: asked only once something is typed, it matches its items with the app's matcher and weights and hands
                back only its best <code>maxResults</code> (50), under its own heading - right for a catalog of thousands of records where only
                what was searched for should show.
                <br />
                The numbers below are measured here, in your browser. Pick a size: the inline palette holds that many synthetic commands -
                type in it. "Measure searches" times the matcher and the ranker the palette runs, over the same commands (the median of 5 runs
                per query), and one whole <code>SearchAsync</code> of a static source over them. "Register them with the service" adds them to the
                modal palette's registry as well (and times it); they are removed when you unregister them or leave the page.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="d-toolbar">
                    <BitChoiceGroup Label="Commands"
                                    Horizontal
                                    TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<int>"
                                    TValue="int"
                                    Disabled="dPerfRegistration is not null"
                                    @bind-Value="dPerfCount"
                                    @bind-Value:after="DBuildPerfCommands">
                        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="500" Value="500" />
                        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="2,000" Value="2000" />
                        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="10,000" Value="10000" />
                    </BitChoiceGroup>
                    <BitToggle Label="Virtualize" @bind-Value="dPerfVirtualize" />
                </div>
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitButton Disabled="dPerfBusy" OnClick="DMeasureAsync">@(dPerfBusy ? "Measuring…" : "Measure searches")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="DTogglePerfRegistration">@(dPerfRegistration is null ? "Register them with the service" : "Unregister them")</BitButton>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Disabled="dPerfRegistration is null" OnClick="@(() => paletteService.OpenAsync("cmd 1234"))">Open the modal with "cmd 1234"</BitButton>
                </div>
                @if (dPerfRegisterTime is { } took)
                {
                    <BitText>The last registration took @DMs(took).</BitText>
                }
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="dPerfCommands"
                                   Virtualize="dPerfVirtualize"
                                   Placeholder="Try c12, cmd 1234 or ^command 7…" />
                @if (dPerfResults.Count > 0)
                {
                    <div class="d-scroll">
                        <table class="d-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Query</th>
                                    <th class="d-num">Matched</th>
                                    <th class="d-num">Match</th>
                                    <th class="d-num">Rank</th>
                                    <th class="d-num">Total</th>
                                    <th class="d-num">Static source</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @foreach (var result in dPerfResults)
                                {
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>@result.Query</code></td>
                                        <td class="d-num">@result.Matched.ToString("N0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</td>
                                        <td class="d-num">@DMs(result.Match)</td>
                                        <td class="d-num">@DMs(result.Rank)</td>
                                        <td class="d-num"><b>@DMs(result.Match + result.Rank)</b></td>
                                        <td class="d-num">@DMs(result.Source)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Keyboard inside the palette" RazorCode="@example36RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example36CsharpCode" Id="example36">
            <div>
                The palette is built to be driven without a mouse: inside it every key has a job. <b>Mod</b> below is <code>Ctrl</code>
                on Windows and Linux and <code>⌘</code> on macOS and iOS.
                <br />
                <table class="e-keys">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Mod</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> and the other toggle shortcuts</td><td>Open or close the modal palette (see "The toggle shortcut" below).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Move the active row, wrapping around at either end unless <code>Loop</code> is off. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> / <kbd>P</kbd> work on every platform, also on a Mac.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>J</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd></td><td>Move down / up, when <code>VimBindings</code> is on.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Home</kbd> <kbd>End</kbd></td><td>Jump to the first or the last row.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>PgUp</kbd> <kbd>PgDn</kbd></td><td>Move ten rows up or down, stopping at the ends.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Run the active command, open its page, flip a toggle, check a row of a multi-select page, submit a prompt, or arm and then confirm a destructive command. On a page that failed to load it retries.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Space</kbd> (empty search box)</td><td>Check or uncheck a row of a multi-select page.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Mod</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd> <kbd>↩</kbd>)</td><td>Apply a multi-select page, run the active row's first secondary action, or open a plain link in a new tab (as GitHub's palette does).</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>→</kbd> (empty search box), a right click</td><td>Open the action panel of the active row: its own actions, then "Pin to top" / "Unpin", "Remove from recents" and "Clear recent commands".</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Backspace</kbd>, <kbd>←</kbd> (empty search box)</td><td>Go back one page.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Disarm a confirmation, then go back a page, then close the modal palette. An Inline palette clears its query instead of closing.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Mod</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Pin or unpin the active row; pinned commands are listed first on the empty query.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>Del</kbd></td><td>Remove the active row from the recents.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <br />
                These keys are claimed in the browser before Blazor sees them, so <kbd>Tab</kbd> never leaves the modal dialog and
                <kbd>Backspace</kbd> never deletes text it should not, even over a slow Blazor Server connection. In right-to-left text
                <kbd>←</kbd> and <kbd>→</kbd> trade places: <kbd>→</kbd> points back to the start of the line, so it goes back a page,
                and <kbd>←</kbd> opens the action panel. Inside the open modal palette its own chords win over a toggle bound to the same
                keys: with Vim bindings on, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> moves up rather than closing it, and <kbd>Mod</kbd>
                <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd> pins even when it is also a toggle shortcut.
                <br />
                An Inline palette is a part of the page, not a dialog, so a few keys behave as they do anywhere else on it: <kbd>Tab</kbd>
                moves the focus on (open the action panel with <kbd>→</kbd> or a right click there), and the page's modal palette keeps
                its toggle - with the default <kbd>Mod</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd>, pressing <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> in the Inline palette
                below opens the modal one on Windows and Linux even with <code>VimBindings</code> on; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>J</kbd> and
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> / <kbd>P</kbd> are always the Inline palette's.
                <br />
                Three parameters tune the navigation of one palette, and the options of the same names
                (<code>BitCommandPaletteOptions.Loop</code>, default true, and <code>VimBindings</code>, default false) set them for all:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Loop</b>: arrowing past the last row comes back to the first. Off, the arrows stop at the ends.</li>
                    <li><b>VimBindings</b>: adds <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>J</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> to the always-on <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd>.</li>
                    <li><b>DisablePointerSelection</b>: hovering a row no longer makes it the active one - only the keys and a click
                        do. Without it, hover moves the active row only after the pointer actually moves, so a list that scrolls under a
                        resting pointer never steals the selection from the keyboard. Clicking a row leaves the focus in the search box.</li>
                </ul>
                Flip them below and try every key: the list has a page (Folders), a multi-select page (Label the thread), a command
                with secondary actions (Open the inbox), a destructive one (Empty the trash) and enough rows for <kbd>PgDn</kbd>. Pin a
                row and clear the query to see it listed first; the right-to-left switch shows the arrows trading places.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="e-toolbar">
                    <BitToggle Label="Loop" @bind-Value="eLoop" />
                    <BitToggle Label="VimBindings" @bind-Value="eVimBindings" />
                    <BitToggle Label="DisablePointerSelection" @bind-Value="eDisablePointerSelection" />
                    <BitToggle Label="Right to left" @bind-Value="eRtl" />
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="eKeyCommands"
                                   Loop="eLoop"
                                   VimBindings="eVimBindings"
                                   DisablePointerSelection="eDisablePointerSelection"
                                   Dir="@(eRtl ? BitDir.Rtl : BitDir.Ltr)" />
                <br />
                <BitText>@eKeyStatus</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Global shortcuts" RazorCode="@example37RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example37CsharpCode" Id="example37">
            <div>
                A command's <b>Shortcut</b> also runs it while the palette is closed, from anywhere on the page - a chord such as
                <code>Alt+Shift+N</code> or a sequence such as <code>G N</code> (<kbd>G</kbd>, then <kbd>N</kbd>). Register such commands
                through <code>IBitCommandPaletteService</code> (or with <code>BitCommandPaletteCommands</code>, for the commands of one
                page): the modal palette on the page claims their keys, and while there is no modal palette - or only Inline ones,
                which claim no global keys - the shortcuts do nothing, so nothing ever runs twice.
                <br />
                The registered shortcuts are synced to the browser as strings and matched there, so only a completed shortcut crosses
                the interop boundary - typing on the page never causes a round trip, even on Blazor Server. Whatever needs the palette
                opens it: a command with a page (<code>G C</code> below opens on its colors), one with a confirmation
                (<code>Alt+Shift+D</code>), and a handler that answers with a page, a prompt or a toast that stays open. A confirmation
                started this way gets a page of its own, so the second <kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms that command and no other. Only a
                command that can run claims its keys: the shortcut of a disabled command, or of one its <code>VisibleWhen</code>
                predicate hides, is left to the page - call <code>paletteService.Refresh()</code> after the state such a predicate
                reads changed.
                <br />
                <b>Shortcut(text, global: false)</b> keeps a shortcut to the palette's rows: it is shown as a hint on the row (Preview
                the note shows <kbd>Space</kbd> in the modal palette), but claims no key on the page - for keys the page or a
                component already handles itself.
                <br />
                <b>Typing is not a shortcut.</b> A plain key pressed while an input, a text area, a select or a content-editable
                element has the focus is typing, so <kbd>G</kbd> then <kbd>N</kbd> in the text field below does nothing; a chord with
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>, <kbd>Alt</kbd> or <kbd>⌘</kbd> still fires there, because nobody types those by accident. Set
                <code>BitCommandPaletteOptions.SuppressShortcutsInEditableElements</code> (default true) to false to let plain keys fire
                from text fields too.
                <br />
                The table below reads the registry right now: it lists every command with a global shortcut that this page's sections
                have registered, and updates through the service's <code>CommandsChanged</code> event. Press one with the palette closed.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <table class="e-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Command</th><th>Kind</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var command in paletteService.Commands.Where(c => c.Shortcut is not null && c.GlobalShortcut))
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="command.Shortcut" MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac" /></td>
                                <td>@command.Title</td>
                                <td>@(command.Shortcut!.IsSequence ? "sequence" : "chord")</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <br />
                <BitText>Last run by a shortcut: <b>@(eShortcutLast ?? "nothing yet")</b></BitText>
                <br />
                <BitTextField Label="Typing is not a shortcut" Placeholder="Type g then n here…" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Keyboard layouts, held keys and input methods" RazorCode="@example38RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example38CsharpCode" Id="example38">
            <div>
                Shortcuts are written once, with Latin letters, and keep working whatever the keyboard types:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Non-Latin layouts.</b> A shortcut is matched on the character the key types and, when that is not an ASCII
                        character, on the key it sits on as well (<code>KeyboardEvent.code</code>, as VS Code does): <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>
                        <kbd>K</kbd> works when a Russian layout types <code>л</code>, <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> when a Persian one
                        types <code>ل</code> <code>د</code>, and the same holds for Arabic, Greek and every other layout whose letters
                        are not ASCII. A Latin layout such as Dvorak or AZERTY keeps its own letters: there <code>Mod+Q</code> is the key
                        that types Q.</li>
                    <li><b>Held keys.</b> A held key's repeats are claimed - the browser does not act on them - but run nothing, so a
                        held <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> does not flicker the palette open and shut, and a held shortcut does not run
                        its command over and over.</li>
                    <li><b>AltGr and Option.</b> A character typed with AltGr, or with Option or Alt alone (<code>Ω</code> for
                        Option+Z on a Mac, <code>@@</code> for AltGr+Q on a German layout), is typing when it lands in a text field, not
                        the shortcut its physical key would make.</li>
                    <li><b>Input methods.</b> Keys that belong to an input method's composition (<code>isComposing</code>, or
                        <code>keyCode</code> 229) are never shortcuts, inside the palette or out, so confirming a Japanese or Chinese
                        candidate with <kbd>Enter</kbd> does not run the active row.</li>
                </ul>
                Nothing needs to be configured for any of this. To try it, switch the operating system to a Russian, Persian, Arabic
                or Greek layout and press the shortcuts of the previous section with the palette closed - <kbd>Alt</kbd>
                <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd>, or <kbd>G</kbd> then <kbd>N</kbd> - or hold one down.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>Last run by a shortcut: <b>@(eShortcutLast ?? "nothing yet")</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Sequences in progress" RazorCode="@example39RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example39CsharpCode" Id="example39">
            <div>
                After the first chords of a sequence - <kbd>G</kbd> of <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd> - the modal palette shows a small status
                near the bottom of the page with the keys pressed so far and says the next one is awaited, as VS Code does in its
                status bar; screen readers announce it. It goes when the sequence completes, breaks off or times out. Press
                <kbd>G</kbd> with the palette closed (and the focus outside a text field) to see it.
                <br />
                <ul>
                    <li><b>SequenceTimeoutMs</b> (<code>BitCommandPaletteOptions</code>, default 1000): how long a sequence waits for its
                        next chord before it breaks off. It must be positive; zero or less throws an
                        <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>ShowSequenceHint</b> (default true): set it to false to show nothing - for an app that shows the pending keys
                        itself.</li>
                    <li><b>IBitCommandPaletteService.SequencePending</b> reports the sequence to code either way: raised with the chords
                        pressed so far, and again with null when it completed, broke off or timed out. The status line below listens to
                        it.</li>
                </ul>
                The built-in status is the <code>bit-cpl-sequence</code> element (<code>data-bit-cpl="sequence"</code>, with
                <code>data-pending="true"</code> while it shows), and its words are <code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.SequencePending</code>
                ("waiting for the next key…") and <code>ShortcutThen</code> ("then").
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>
                    Sequence:
                    <b>@(eSequencePending is null ? "none in progress" : $"{eSequencePending.ToDisplayString(paletteService.IsMac)}, waiting for the next key")</b>
                </BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Shortcut conflicts" RazorCode="@example40RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example40CsharpCode" Id="example40">
            <div>
                When several commands claim one global shortcut, the one registered last wins - so a page's own <code>Mod+S</code>
                takes over from the layout's while the page is shown, and gives the keys back when it is disposed. The modal palette
                checks the shortcuts whenever the registry changes and logs a warning through <code>ILogger</code> (the browser's
                console on WebAssembly, the server's log on Blazor Server), once for each problem:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>A duplicate:</b> a shortcut several commands claim - the warning names them all and the one that runs.</li>
                    <li><b>Shadowed by the toggle:</b> a command's shortcut that is also a toggle shortcut of the palette, which takes
                        it, so the command never runs from it.</li>
                    <li><b>An unreachable sequence:</b> one whose first chords are a shortcut of their own and fire first -
                        <code>G</code> registered next to <code>G I</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>A browser-reserved key:</b> one browsers keep for themselves and never hand to a page - <code>Mod+W</code>,
                        <code>Mod+T</code>, <code>Mod+N</code>, their <code>Shift</code> variants, <code>Mod+Q</code>,
                        <code>Ctrl+Tab</code>, <code>Alt+F4</code>.</li>
                </ul>
                Two commands registered together with the same id are reported too: the second replaces the first, so give commands
                built from data explicit ids with <code>.Id(...)</code>. These configuration warnings always name the commands, even with
                <code>RecordCommandIdsInTraces</code> off. Inline palettes claim no global keys, so they report none of this.
                <br />
                This page registers "Save draft" on <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Q</kbd>. Register "Save and publish" on the same keys and, with
                the palette closed, <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Q</kbd> runs it instead - the later registration wins - while the log shows the
                palette's warning naming both. Dispose it and the keys are "Save draft"'s again.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="EToggleConflict">@EConflictButtonText</BitButton>
                <br />
                <br />
                <BitText>
                    <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Q</kbd> runs <b>@(eConflictOverride is null ? "Save draft" : "Save and publish")</b>,
                    last pressed: <b>@(eConflictLast ?? "not yet")</b>
                </BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Shortcut syntax" RazorCode="@example41RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example41CsharpCode" Id="example41">
            <div>
                A shortcut is a <code>BitCommandPaletteKeyboardShortcut</code>: one or more <code>BitCommandPaletteKeyChord</code>s
                pressed in order. Write it as text: chords are separated by spaces and keys inside a chord by <code>+</code> -
                <code>Mod+K</code>, <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code>, <code>G I</code>, <code>Alt+Z</code>, <code>F2</code>,
                <code>Shift+?</code>. A string converts implicitly wherever a shortcut is expected (<code>.Shortcut("G I")</code>, the
                options' <code>ToggleShortcut</code>, the <code>Shortcut</code> of <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code>).
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Mod</b> is <kbd>⌘</kbd> on macOS and iOS and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> elsewhere - the one to use for an app's own
                        shortcuts (<code>CmdOrCtrl</code> and <code>CommandOrControl</code> mean the same). <code>Ctrl</code> is
                        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> (<kbd>⌃</kbd>) everywhere, and <code>Meta</code> is <kbd>⌘</kbd> on a Mac and <kbd>Win</kbd>
                        elsewhere.</li>
                    <li>Modifiers have aliases: <code>Control</code> and <code>⌃</code>; <code>⇧</code>; <code>Option</code>,
                        <code>Opt</code> and <code>⌥</code> for <code>Alt</code>; <code>Cmd</code>, <code>Command</code>,
                        <code>Win</code>, <code>Super</code> and <code>⌘</code> for <code>Meta</code>.</li>
                    <li>So do keys: <code>Esc</code>, <code>Return</code>, <code>Space</code> (or <code>Spacebar</code>),
                        <code>Del</code>, <code>Ins</code>, <code>Up</code>/<code>Down</code>/<code>Left</code>/<code>Right</code>,
                        <code>PgUp</code>, <code>PgDn</code>, <code>Plus</code>, <code>Minus</code>, <code>Comma</code>,
                        <code>Period</code>, <code>Slash</code>, <code>Backslash</code>. Any other key is the browser's
                        <code>KeyboardEvent.key</code> name (<code>F2</code>, <code>Home</code>, <code>Tab</code>), matched
                        case-insensitively. A shifted character also fires without its <code>Shift</code>: <code>?</code> fires on
                        <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>/</kbd> of a US layout.</li>
                    <li><code>BitCommandPaletteKeyboardShortcut.Parse</code> throws a <code>FormatException</code> for a chord without a
                        key (<code>Ctrl+Shift</code>) or with two (<code>A+B</code>); <code>TryParse</code> returns false instead.</li>
                    <li>Two shortcuts are equal when their <code>Normalized</code> forms are - lower case, modifiers in a fixed order
                        (<code>mod+ctrl+alt+shift+meta+key</code>) - so <code>Ctrl+K</code> equals <code>control+k</code>.
                        <code>IsSequence</code> and <code>Chords</code> tell a sequence from a chord, <code>ToDisplayString(isMac)</code>
                        spells it out ("Ctrl+Shift+K", "G then I") and <code>DisplayTokens(isMac)</code> gives the key caps a hint
                        renders.</li>
                </ul>
                Type one, or pick a sample:
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitTextField Label="Shortcut" @bind-Value="eShortcutText" Immediate />
                <div class="e-toolbar e-chips">
                    @foreach (var sample in eShortcutSamples)
                    {
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => eShortcutText = sample">@sample</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                @if (BitCommandPaletteKeyboardShortcut.TryParse(eShortcutText, out var eParsed))
                {
                    <table class="e-table">
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><th>On macOS</th><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eParsed" MacKeys="true" /></td></tr>
                            <tr><th>Elsewhere</th><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eParsed" MacKeys="false" /></td></tr>
                            <tr><th>In words</th><td>@eParsed.ToDisplayString(isMac: false)</td></tr>
                            <tr><th>Normalized</th><td><code>@eParsed.Normalized</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><th>Kind</th><td>@(eParsed.IsSequence ? $"a sequence of {eParsed.Chords.Count} chords" : "a chord")</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                }
                else
                {
                    <BitText>Not a shortcut: <code>TryParse</code> returns false, and <code>Parse</code> would throw a <code>FormatException</code>.</BitText>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Platform-aware hints" RazorCode="@example42RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example42CsharpCode" Id="example42">
            <div>
                The palette detects macOS and iOS once it starts in the browser, and every hint it draws follows: <kbd>⌘</kbd>
                <kbd>⇧</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> there, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> elsewhere - and a sequence reads
                <kbd>G</kbd> then <kbd>S</kbd> on both.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>IBitCommandPaletteService.IsMac</b> exposes the result to your own UI. It is false until the palette has
                        started, and the service's <b>PlatformDetected</b> event says when it is known: subscribe to it to render
                        again (and unsubscribe in <code>Dispose</code> - the service outlives the page).</li>
                    <li><b>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</b> renders any shortcut as the palette does: key caps in
                        <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code> elements, laid out left to right even in right-to-left text, as on the keys themselves,
                        and hidden from screen readers, which read its words instead ("Ctrl+Shift+K", "G then S"). Its <b>MacKeys</b>
                        parameter picks the macOS symbols (pass <code>paletteService.IsMac</code> to follow the platform), and
                        <b>Strings</b> the word between the chords of a sequence (<code>ShortcutThen</code>). <code>Class</code> and
                        <code>Style</code> style it.</li>
                    <li><b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b> follows the detected platform by itself; its <b>MacKeys</b> parameter forces the
                        symbols on or off.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>This browser is <b>@(paletteService.IsMac ? "a Mac" : "not a Mac")</b>.</BitText>
                <br />
                <table class="e-table">
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th>This platform</th><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintChord" MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac" /></td><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintSequence" MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac" /></td></tr>
                        <tr><th>MacKeys</th><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintChord" MacKeys="true" /></td><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintSequence" MacKeys="true" /></td></tr>
                        <tr><th>MacKeys="false"</th><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintChord" MacKeys="false" /></td><td><BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="eHintSequence" MacKeys="false" /></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <br />
                <div class="e-toolbar">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger MacKeys="true" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger MacKeys="false" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="The toggle shortcut" RazorCode="@example43RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example43CsharpCode" Id="example43">
            <div>
                The toggle shortcut opens the modal palette when it is closed and closes it when it is open.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>ToggleShortcut</b> (<code>BitCommandPaletteOptions</code>, default <code>Mod+K</code>: <kbd>⌘</kbd>
                        <kbd>K</kbd> on a Mac, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> elsewhere) is the main one, and the one
                        <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code> shows.</li>
                    <li><b>AdditionalToggleShortcuts</b> adds more keys that do the same - <code>Mod+Shift+P</code> for people used to
                        VS Code, or <code>/</code>; a sequence works too.</li>
                    <li>The palette's <b>ToggleShortcut</b> parameter overrides the option's main shortcut for that palette
                        (<code>ToggleShortcut="Mod+P"</code>); a value that does not parse is ignored and the option applies. The
                        additional shortcuts always come from the options.</li>
                    <li><b>A toggle of null</b>: set <code>options.ToggleShortcut = null</code> for no main toggle shortcut - the palette
                        then opens only from <code>AdditionalToggleShortcuts</code>, a <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code>, its bound
                        <code>Open</code> or the service's <code>OpenAsync</code> / <code>ToggleAsync</code> - for an app whose own
                        editor uses <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd>.</li>
                </ul>
                Only the modal palette answers the toggle: an Inline palette, or a <code>Disabled</code> one, claims none. While the
                palette is open only single-chord toggles stay global, and its own chords win over a toggle bound to the same keys -
                with Vim bindings on, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>K</kbd> moves up inside the palette rather than closing it. A command
                shortcut that is also a toggle shortcut never runs, and the palette logs it (see Shortcut conflicts).
                <br />
                The toggle shortcuts of this site, read from the injected options:
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="e-toolbar">
                    @foreach (var toggle in EToggleShortcuts)
                    {
                        <BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="toggle" MacKeys="paletteService.IsMac" />
                    }
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => paletteService.ToggleAsync()">paletteService.ToggleAsync()</BitButton>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Options" RazorCode="@example44RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example44CsharpCode" Id="example44">
            <div>
                <b>BitCommandPaletteOptions</b> is the app-wide configuration, set once in <code>Program.cs</code> through
                <code>builder.Services.AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices(commandPalette: options =&gt; ...)</code> - in both the server and the client project
                of a Blazor Web App. The options are a singleton shared by every palette and every user of the app; the registry, the
                open state and the history are scoped (per circuit on Blazor Server, per tab on WebAssembly). Numbers that must be
                positive, or not negative, throw an <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</code> when set otherwise, so a bad value fails at
                startup. What each group is for:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Keyboard</b> - <code>ToggleShortcut</code>, <code>AdditionalToggleShortcuts</code>,
                        <code>SuppressShortcutsInEditableElements</code>, <code>SequenceTimeoutMs</code>, <code>ShowSequenceHint</code>,
                        <code>Loop</code>, <code>VimBindings</code>: the sections above.</li>
                    <li><b>Search and ranking</b> - <code>Matcher</code>, <code>Weights</code>, <code>MaxResults</code>,
                        <code>GroupWhenSearching</code>, <code>GroupOrder</code>, <code>PriorityWeight</code>,
                        <code>FrecencyWeight</code>, <code>SearchNestedCommands</code>, <code>TypoTolerance</code>,
                        <code>HighlightMatches</code>: how a query is matched, ranked and shown.</li>
                    <li><b>Modes and sources</b> - <code>DebounceMs</code>, <code>SourceTimeoutMs</code>, <code>ShowModeHints</code>:
                        how async sources are asked and when they count as failed.</li>
                    <li><b>Registered at startup</b> - <code>Commands</code>, <code>Modes</code>, <code>Sources</code>: what the
                        registry holds before any page registers anything.</li>
                    <li><b>History</b> - <code>EnableHistory</code>, <code>MaxRecents</code>, <code>MaxHistoryEntries</code>,
                        <code>HistoryStorageKey</code>: recents, pins and frecency (see History storage).</li>
                    <li><b>Display and behavior</b> - <code>CloseOnBackdropClick</code>, <code>CloseOnNavigation</code>,
                        <code>ResetOnClose</code> (false reopens where it was left), <code>ShowFooter</code>,
                        <code>ShowShortcuts</code>, <code>Virtualize</code>, <code>RowHeight</code>, <code>Strings</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>Security and diagnostics</b> - <code>AllowedUrlSchemes</code>, <code>RecordCommandIdsInTraces</code> (see
                        Security).</li>
                </ul>
                The look is not an option: the palette follows the BlazorUI theme (a <code>bit-theme="dark"</code> attribute on it or an
                ancestor, or a <code>BitThemeProvider</code>), and its density follows <code>--bit-layout-density-scale</code> (e.g.
                <code>0.8</code> on its <code>Style</code> or an ancestor). The full table of options, with types and defaults, is in the
                <code>BitCommandPaletteOptions</code> sub-class below.
                <br />
                The Inline palette below searches the options themselves, each listed with the value this site runs with - type
                <code>history</code> or <code>shortcut</code>. It is driven through the component rather than the service: a
                <code>@@ref</code> to call <b>SetQueryAsync</b> (sets the query as if typed) from the buttons, and
                <b>OnQueryChanged</b> to follow what is typed. The component also exposes <code>IsOpen</code>, <code>Query</code> and
                <code>ActiveCommand</code>, and <code>OpenAsync</code>, <code>CloseAsync</code> and <code>ToggleAsync</code> - the same as
                the service's, for the code that holds a reference to one palette.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="e-toolbar">
                    @foreach (var query in eFinderQueries)
                    {
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="() => eFinder.SetQueryAsync(query)">SetQueryAsync("@query")</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette @ref="eFinder"
                                   Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Placeholder="Search the options…"
                                   Commands="eOptionCommands"
                                   OnQueryChanged="query => eFinderQuery = query" />
                <br />
                <BitText>
                    OnQueryChanged: <b>@(eFinderQuery.Length == 0 ? "(empty)" : eFinderQuery)</b>,
                    picked: <b>@(eOptionPicked ?? "nothing yet")</b>
                </BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Options, parameters and BitParams" RazorCode="@example45RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example45CsharpCode" Id="example45">
            <div>
                A palette's settings come from three places, and the nearest wins:
                <ol>
                    <li><b>The palette's own parameter</b> - <code>&lt;BitCommandPalette Loop="false" /&gt;</code> - always wins.</li>
                    <li><b>A BitCommandPaletteParams cascaded through a BitParams</b>: every palette under the <code>BitParams</code> takes
                        each parameter it did not set itself from it - the defaults of one area of an app, such as an admin section or
                        the Inline palettes of a settings page. A nested <code>BitParams</code> refines the one around it: it replaces
                        only what it sets, unless it is <code>Isolated</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>BitCommandPaletteOptions</b>, the app-wide default, for whatever neither of the above set.</li>
                </ol>
                <code>BitCommandPaletteParams</code> carries what the palettes of an app agree on - their look and words and how they
                search and navigate: <code>Classes</code>, <code>Styles</code>, <code>Placeholder</code>, <code>Strings</code>,
                <code>DisablePointerSelection</code>, and the option overrides <code>EnableHistory</code>,
                <code>GroupWhenSearching</code>, <code>HighlightMatches</code>, <code>Loop</code>, <code>SearchNestedCommands</code>,
                <code>ShowFooter</code>, <code>ShowShortcuts</code>, <code>TypoTolerance</code>, <code>VimBindings</code> and
                <code>Virtualize</code>, plus the parameters every BlazorUI component shares (<code>Class</code>, <code>Style</code>,
                <code>Dir</code>...). What makes one palette itself - <code>Inline</code>, <code>Open</code>, its
                <code>Commands</code>, templates and callbacks - is not cascaded. The option overrides are nullable: left unset, on the
                palette and in the params, the option applies.
                <br />
                Both palettes below sit in one <code>BitParams</code> that sets a placeholder, hides the footer, turns highlighting and
                history off and Vim bindings on. The first takes all of it; the second sets <code>Placeholder</code>,
                <code>ShowFooter</code> and <code>HighlightMatches</code> itself, and those win. Type "an" in both. <code>Loop</code>,
                set by neither, comes from the options.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitParams Parameters="eCascadedParams">
                    <div class="e-pair">
                        <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" Commands="eCascadeCommands" />
                        <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                           IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                           Commands="eCascadeCommands"
                                           Placeholder="Its own placeholder wins…"
                                           ShowFooter="true"
                                           HighlightMatches="true" />
                    </div>
                </BitParams>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Security" RazorCode="@example46RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example46CsharpCode" Id="example46">
            <div>
                Commands often carry data from an API - an async source's results, a page loaded on demand - so the palette treats what
                it shows and follows as untrusted:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Text is text.</b> Titles, subtitles, descriptions, badges, values, queries and whatever a source returns are
                        rendered as text by Razor, never as HTML: the second row below has a title made of an <code>&lt;img
                        onerror&gt;</code> tag, shown as the characters it is. Icons are <code>RenderFragment</code>s you build, or a CSS
                        class name.</li>
                    <li><b>Links are checked.</b> A command's <code>Href</code> is followed only when it is relative or its scheme is in
                        <b>AllowedUrlSchemes</b> (<code>BitCommandPaletteOptions</code>; <code>http</code>, <code>https</code>,
                        <code>mailto</code> and <code>tel</code> by default, compared case-insensitively). A <code>javascript:</code>,
                        <code>data:</code> or <code>vbscript:</code> link - read the way a browser reads it, tabs and control characters
                        included - is refused: the palette shows its generic failure message and logs a warning naming the command and
                        the scheme. Choose the first row below: nothing runs. Add a scheme for links an app trusts
                        (<code>options.AllowedUrlSchemes.Add("vscode")</code>) or remove one it does not need.</li>
                    <li><b>Errors are generic.</b> A failed command shows <code>BitCommandPaletteStrings.CommandFailed</code> ("Couldn't
                        run “…”"), never the exception's text, which can name a server, a table or a user; the exception goes to the
                        log and to the service's <code>CommandFailed</code> event.</li>
                    <li><b>Visibility is not authorization.</b> <code>VisibleWhen</code> and <code>Disabled</code> only shape the UI.
                        Guards are asked every time a command is about to run - from a row, a shortcut or code - and refuse it when they
                        say so (see the Guards section). On WebAssembly they run in the browser, so the server must still check every
                        action a handler asks it to perform.</li>
                    <li><b>Ids and titles.</b> The history stores command ids, use counts and times - never queries, titles or prompt
                        values - and traces carry the id of the command that ran. Keep personal data out of ids: use an opaque id
                        (<code>person:1042</code>, not the e-mail address), and mark a command built from personal data
                        <code>ExcludeFromHistory()</code>, as the third row below is. Titles are shown, searched and announced, but never
                        stored or traced.</li>
                    <li><b>RecordCommandIdsInTraces</b> (default true): set it to false when ids embed personal data anyway. The
                        <code>bit.command_palette.command</code> activities then carry no id, and the log messages about running a
                        command - a failure, a refusal, a guard that threw, an unsafe link - read <code>(hidden)</code> instead of its id
                        and title. Metrics never carry ids; the configuration warnings about shortcuts and duplicate ids still name the
                        commands an app registers.</li>
                    <li><b>Stored history is validated</b> when it is read: entries a hand-edited or corrupt <code>localStorage</code>
                        holds are dropped.</li>
                    <li><b>Search is bounded.</b> A query longer than <code>BitCommandPaletteQuery.MaxLength</code> (256) characters
                        matches nothing instead of being searched, and a field longer than
                        <code>BitCommandPaletteFuzzyMatcher.MaxOptimalLength</code> is aligned greedily in one pass, so a pasted wall of
                        text cannot make a keystroke expensive. Search work still grows with the fields you give it: keep descriptions to
                        what a row needs, and search large documents through an async source backed by a real index.</li>
                    <li><b>No inline script.</b> The palette's browser layer adds no inline scripts and uses no <code>eval</code>, so a
                        content security policy needs nothing extra for it.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="eSecurityCommands"
                                   OnCommandExecuted="e => eSecurityLast = e.Command.Title" />
                <br />
                <BitText>Last run: <b>@(eSecurityLast ?? "nothing yet")</b></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="History storage" RazorCode="@example47RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example47CsharpCode" Id="example47">
            <div>
                Every run is recorded - unless the command has <code>ExcludeFromHistory</code> - as the command's id, how often it ran,
                when it last ran, and whether and when it was pinned (<code>BitCommandPaletteCommandUsage</code>). The counts feed the
                recents and pins on the empty query and the frecency that nudges ranking. The history is scoped - one per circuit on
                Blazor Server, one per tab on WebAssembly - and shared by every palette in it, modal and Inline alike, keyed by command
                id: give the commands of different palettes different ids.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>The default store</b> keeps it in the browser's <code>localStorage</code> under
                        <b>HistoryStorageKey</b> (<code>BitCommandPaletteOptions</code>, default <code>bit-command-palette.history</code>),
                        so recents survive a reload. Change the key to keep two apps on one origin apart. While prerendering, with
                        storage disabled or with corrupt data, it starts empty, and saving is best-effort.</li>
                    <li><b>AddBitCommandPaletteInMemoryHistoryStore()</b> keeps it in memory only, for the lifetime of the scope, so
                        nothing is written to the browser - for shared machines or kiosks.</li>
                    <li><b>AddBitCommandPaletteHistoryStore&lt;T&gt;()</b> replaces the store with your own
                        <code>IBitCommandPaletteHistoryStore</code> - for example per user, on the server, so a person's recents and pins
                        follow them from device to device. It has two methods, <code>LoadAsync</code> (once, when a palette first needs
                        the history) and <code>SaveAsync</code> (after every change, with the whole history); it is resolved from the
                        scope, so it can take the signed-in user's services. A store that throws is logged; the palette then starts
                        empty, or keeps the change for the session only.</li>
                    <li><b>MaxRecents</b> (default 5) is how many recent commands the empty query lists, and <b>MaxHistoryEntries</b>
                        (default 200) how many commands the history keeps at most: past it, the least recently used are pruned, pinned
                        commands never. Both must not be negative.</li>
                    <li><b>EnableHistory</b> (default true) turns it all off - for every palette with the option, for one with the
                        parameter or through <code>BitCommandPaletteParams</code>.</li>
                </ul>
                The action panel of any row also offers "Clear recent commands", which forgets every use and keeps the pins. Run a few
                commands below, pin one with <kbd>Mod</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd>, then read what the default store wrote - or
                reload the page and clear the query: the recents are still there.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   Commands="eHistoryCommands"
                                   Placeholder="Run a few, then clear the query…" />
                <br />
                <div class="e-toolbar">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="EReadStoredHistory">Read localStorage</BitButton>
                    <BitText>Key: <code>@eOptions.HistoryStorageKey</code>, MaxRecents: <b>@eOptions.MaxRecents</b>, MaxHistoryEntries: <b>@eOptions.MaxHistoryEntries</b></BitText>
                </div>
                <pre class="e-json">@(eStoredHistory ?? "Not read yet.")</pre>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Item template" RazorCode="@example48RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example48CsharpCode" Id="example48">
            <div>
                <b>ItemTemplate</b> replaces the content of every row. It receives a <b>BitCommandPaletteItemContext</b>:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Row</code>: the <b>BitCommandPaletteRow</b> being drawn. <code>Row.Command</code> is the command;
                        <code>Row.Match</code> says how the query matched it: <code>TitlePositions</code> and <code>SubtitlePositions</code>
                        (the matched character indexes), <code>MatchedKeyword</code> (the keyword that matched when the title did not) and
                        <code>Path</code> (the titles of the pages above a nested command found from the root page, "Theme › Dark").
                        <code>Row.Group</code> is its heading and <code>Row.ItemIndex</code> its position among the rows.</li>
                    <li><code>IsActive</code>: the row the arrow keys are on, which Enter runs.</li>
                    <li><code>IsChecked</code>: the row is checked on a multi-select page.</li>
                    <li><code>IsPinned</code>: the command is pinned to the top of the recents.</li>
                    <li><code>IsConfirming</code>: a destructive command waiting for its second Enter.</li>
                    <li><code>IsMac</code>: the palette runs on a Mac, so key names should be the Mac symbols (⌘, ↩).</li>
                </ul>
                The template replaces everything inside the row: the icon, the title and its highlighting, the subtitle, the badge, the
                toggle's switch, the checkbox of a multi-select page, the shortcut and the chevron of a page. Draw the parts you need.
                The row element itself stays the palette's: its <code>role="option"</code>, its <code>aria-selected</code>,
                <code>aria-checked</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code>, the id that <code>aria-activedescendant</code> points at, its
                <code>data-bit-cpl="item"</code> and state attributes (<code>data-active</code>, <code>data-disabled</code>...), its
                hover and click handling and its <code>Classes.Item</code> / <code>Classes.ActiveItem</code>. A template therefore
                cannot break keyboard navigation or what a screen reader hears about the row.
                <br />
                Two helpers bring back what the default row draws:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText</b> (<code>Text</code>, <code>Positions</code>) renders a text with each run of
                        matched characters in a <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code>, painted with <code>--bit-CommandPalette-mark-background</code>.
                        It widens a match to whole grapheme clusters, so an accent or half of an emoji is never split off. Pass
                        <code>Row.Match?.TitlePositions</code> for the title and <code>SubtitlePositions</code> for the subtitle.</li>
                    <li><b>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</b> (<code>Shortcut</code>, <code>MacKeys</code>, <code>Strings</code>,
                        <code>Class</code>, <code>Style</code>) renders a shortcut as key caps, in the platform's symbols when
                        <code>MacKeys</code> is set. The caps always read left to right and are hidden from screen readers, which hear the
                        shortcut in words ("Ctrl+K", "G then S"). <code>Strings</code> gives the word between the chords of a sequence.</li>
                </ul>
                Mark the decorative parts of a template <code>aria-hidden</code>, never put a button or a link inside a row (the focus
                stays in the search box and rows are never focused), and draw nothing the palette already says: see Accessibility below.
                <code>EnableHistory="false"</code> keeps this sample's runs out of the recents, so the list stays as it is.
                <br />
                Type <code>cs</code> or <code>src</code> to see the highlighting, and open a file for the toast.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fFiles" Placeholder="Search files…">
                    <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                        <span class="f-row">
                            <span class="f-ext" aria-hidden="true">@FExtension(item.Row.Command)</span>
                            <span class="f-text">
                                <span class="f-title">
                                    <BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@item.Row.Command.Title" Positions="@item.Row.Match?.TitlePositions" />
                                </span>
                                @if (item.Row.Command.Subtitle is not null)
                                {
                                    <span class="f-sub">
                                        <BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@item.Row.Command.Subtitle" Positions="@item.Row.Match?.SubtitlePositions" />
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </span>
                            @if (item.Row.Command.Shortcut is not null)
                            {
                                <BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="item.Row.Command.Shortcut" MacKeys="item.IsMac" />
                            }
                            @if (item.IsActive)
                            {
                                <kbd class="f-go" aria-hidden="true">@(item.IsMac ? "↩" : "Enter")</kbd>
                            }
                        </span>
                    </ItemTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Icon & group header templates" RazorCode="@example49RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example49CsharpCode" Id="example49">
            <div>
                <b>IconTemplate</b> draws the icon of every row and receives the row's <b>BitCommandPaletteCommand</b>. Unlike
                <code>ItemTemplate</code> it leaves the rest of the row to the palette - the highlighted title, the subtitle, the badge, the
                switch, the shortcut and the chevron - and only fills the icon slot, a small square at the start of the row that is hidden
                from screen readers. It wins over a command's own <code>Icon</code> and <code>IconCss</code> and over the icon the palette
                picks for the command's kind; render nothing for a row without an icon. It has no effect while an <code>ItemTemplate</code>
                is set, since that replaces the whole content of the row, icon included.
                <br />
                <b>GroupHeaderTemplate</b> replaces the content of every group heading and receives the heading's text. The heading element
                stays the palette's: it sticks to the top of the list while its group scrolls, carries <code>Classes.GroupHeading</code>,
                and is what the group's <code>role="group"</code> is named by (<code>aria-labelledby</code>), so a screen reader announces
                the group by the text the template renders. Keep the heading's words in it, and hide the decoration from screen readers
                as the sample does with its glyph.
                <br />
                Here the icon is the extension's initial, colored by the extension, and each heading gets a glyph.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fFiles" Placeholder="Search files…">
                    <IconTemplate Context="command">
                        <span class="f-icon" data-ext="@FExtension(command)">@(FExtension(command)[..1].ToUpperInvariant())</span>
                    </IconTemplate>
                    <GroupHeaderTemplate Context="heading">
                        <span class="f-group"><span aria-hidden="true">⌁ </span>@heading</span>
                    </GroupHeaderTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Header & footer templates" RazorCode="@example50RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example50CsharpCode" Id="example50">
            <div>
                <b>HeaderTemplate</b> adds content above the search box - the breadcrumbs and the box stay - and <b>FooterTemplate</b>
                replaces the footer, its keyboard hints and its toasts. The footer template is shown only while the footer is
                (<code>ShowFooter</code>, on by default). Both receive a <b>BitCommandPaletteStateContext</b>, a snapshot of the palette
                taken on every render:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Query</code>: what is searched for, without a mode's prefix; <code>ModePrefix</code> and <code>ModeName</code>:
                        the mode typed, if any.</li>
                    <li><code>Breadcrumbs</code>: the titles of the pages opened, empty on the root page.</li>
                    <li><code>ResultCount</code>, <code>ActiveIndex</code> and <code>ActiveCommand</code>: how many rows are listed and which
                        one is active.</li>
                    <li><code>IsLoading</code>: a lazily loaded page or an async source has not answered yet.</li>
                    <li><code>IsMultiSelect</code> and <code>SelectedCount</code>: a multi-select page and how many rows are checked.</li>
                    <li><code>Toast</code>: the message a command returned, or a confirmation or an error, while it is shown.</li>
                    <li><code>IsMac</code>: whether to name the keys with the Mac symbols.</li>
                </ul>
                The palette's live regions announce every toast whatever the footer shows - its own footer, a <code>FooterTemplate</code>
                or no footer at all - so a footer template that shows <code>Toast</code> gives it no <code>role="status"</code> or
                <code>aria-live</code> of its own, which would have it announced twice. <code>Classes.Header</code> and
                <code>Classes.Footer</code> (see Style &amp; Class) style the palette's own header and footer; a template is styled by
                what it renders.
                <br />
                Here the header counts the files and follows the pages, and the footer shows the active file's path and size, or the toast
                of the file just opened. Open <i>The archive…</i> to see the header follow the page while it loads.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fFiles" Placeholder="Search files…">
                    <HeaderTemplate Context="state">
                        <div class="f-header">
                            @(state.Breadcrumbs.Count > 0 ? string.Join(" › ", state.Breadcrumbs) : "All files")
                            ·
                            @(state.IsLoading ? "loading…" : state.ResultCount == 1 ? "1 file" : $"{state.ResultCount} files")
                            @if (state.Query.Length > 0)
                            {
                                <span> matching “@state.Query”</span>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </HeaderTemplate>
                    <FooterTemplate Context="state">
                        <div class="f-footer">
                            <span class="f-footer-text">
                                @if (state.Toast is not null)
                                {
                                    @state.Toast
                                }
                                else if (state.ActiveCommand?.Data is FFileInfo file)
                                {
                                    @($"{file.Folder}/{file.Name} · {file.Size}")
                                }
                            </span>
                            <span aria-hidden="true"><kbd class="f-go">@(state.IsMac ? "↩" : "Enter")</kbd> to open</span>
                        </div>
                    </FooterTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Detail pane" RazorCode="@example51RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example51CsharpCode" Id="example51">
            <div>
                <b>DetailTemplate</b> adds a pane beside the list for the active command, Raycast style: a preview of a file, a person's
                card, the description of a setting. It receives the active <b>BitCommandPaletteCommand</b> and is shown while a row is
                active, so it follows the arrow keys and the pointer. Whatever a command carries can be shown there: its
                <code>Description</code> (which is searched too, with a low weight), its <code>Data</code> - any object, such as the
                record of the file below - and its <code>Shortcut</code>, through <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code>.
                <br />
                The pane is <code>--bit-CommandPalette-detail-width</code> wide (40% by default) and is styled through
                <code>Classes.Detail</code> / <code>Styles.Detail</code>. A modal palette with a detail pane opens wider, 920px instead of
                640px, for the pane to have room (<code>--bit-CommandPalette-width</code> still decides when it is set); an inline palette
                keeps the width of its container. Below 600px the pane is hidden, as the modal palette then fills the screen.
                <br />
                The pane is beside the list, not in it: a screen reader follows the active option, not the pane. Put what a reader needs
                to choose a command in its title or subtitle, and keep the pane for what adds to them.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fFiles" Placeholder="Search files…">
                    <DetailTemplate Context="command">
                        <div class="f-detail">
                            <b>@command.Title</b>
                            @if (command.Data is FFileInfo file)
                            {
                                <dl>
                                    <dt>Folder</dt>
                                    <dd>@file.Folder</dd>
                                    <dt>Size</dt>
                                    <dd>@file.Size</dd>
                                    <dt>Modified</dt>
                                    <dd>@file.Modified</dd>
                                </dl>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <p>@command.Subtitle</p>
                            }
                            @if (command.Shortcut is not null)
                            {
                                <BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="command.Shortcut" />
                            }
                        </div>
                    </DetailTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Loading, no results & empty" RazorCode="@example52RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example52CsharpCode" Id="example52">
            <div>
                Three states take the place of the list, each with a template and, for the last two, a text:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>LoadingTemplate</b> is shown while a lazily loaded page (<code>Children</code> given a loader) or an async source
                        has nothing to show yet. It replaces the whole default state, a spinner and <code>Strings.Loading</code>, so give
                        what it says a <code>role="status"</code> as the sample does. The search box's icon turns into a spinner as well,
                        and the list is marked <code>aria-busy</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>NoResultsTemplate</b> is shown when a search matched nothing and receives the query. <b>NoResultsText</b>
                        replaces only the sentence, keeping the icon and <code>Strings.NoResultsHint</code> under it. Without either, the
                        palette shows <code>Strings.NoResults</code> with the query in its <code>{0}</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>EmptyTemplate</b> is shown by a page with nothing on it at all, before anything is typed: a folder with no files,
                        a lazy page whose loader returned nothing, a root page whose commands are not registered yet. <b>EmptyText</b>
                        replaces only the sentence. Without either, the palette shows <code>Strings.Empty</code>.</li>
                </ul>
                The template wins over the text, and the text over the strings. The no-results and empty states are rendered inside a
                <code>role="status"</code> element of the palette's (styled by <code>Classes.NoResults</code> and <code>Classes.Empty</code>),
                so their templates need no live role of their own. A template's text is read by screen readers when it appears; the result
                count is announced too, so keep it short.
                <br />
                In the first palette, type <code>zz</code> for the no-results state, open <i>The archive…</i> for the loading state and
                <i>An empty folder</i> for the empty one. The second palette has no commands at all: it shows its <code>EmptyTemplate</code>,
                and its <code>NoResultsText</code> once something is typed.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fFiles" Placeholder="Search files…">
                    <LoadingTemplate>
                        <p class="f-state" role="status">Opening the folder…</p>
                    </LoadingTemplate>
                    <NoResultsTemplate Context="query">
                        <p class="f-state">No file matches “@query”. Try <code>json</code>.</p>
                    </NoResultsTemplate>
                    <EmptyTemplate>
                        <p class="f-state">This folder is empty.</p>
                    </EmptyTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" NoResultsText="There is nothing here to find.">
                    <EmptyTemplate>
                        <p class="f-state">No commands yet. They appear here once they are registered.</p>
                    </EmptyTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Localization" RazorCode="@example53RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example53CsharpCode" Id="example53">
            <div>
                Every word of the palette - the placeholder, the footer's hints, the empty and loading states, the confirmation, and the
                words only screen readers hear, such as the dialog's name, the breadcrumbs' name (<code>Breadcrumb</code>), a switch's
                <code>On</code> / <code>Off</code>, the "then" of <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> (<code>ShortcutThen</code>) and the
                announcement of a page (<code>PageAnnouncement</code>) - is a property of <b>BitCommandPaletteStrings</b>. To translate them:
                <ul>
                    <li>For one palette, set its <b>Strings</b> parameter, as the palette below does.</li>
                    <li>For every palette, set <code>options.Strings</code> in the <code>commandPalette</code> argument of <code>AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices</code>. The
                        <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code>s and <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code>s outside a palette read these
                        too; a <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code> also takes a <code>Strings</code> parameter of its own.</li>
                    <li>For an app serving several languages, subclass it: every property is <code>virtual</code>, so a subclass can read
                        them from an <code>IStringLocalizer</code> - the usual resource files - for the culture of each request.</li>
                </ul>
                Some strings are composite format strings: <code>{0}</code> (and <code>{1}</code> in <code>PageAnnouncement</code> and
                <code>ApproximateResultsAnnouncement</code>) is filled in with <code>string.Format</code> in the current culture
                (<code>CultureInfo.CurrentCulture</code>) - the query in <code>NoResults</code>, the count in <code>ResultsAnnouncement</code>,
                <code>SelectedCount</code> and <code>MoreResults</code>, the command's title in <code>CommandFailed</code>,
                <code>CommandRefused</code> and <code>CommandUnavailable</code>, the source's name in <code>SourceFailed</code>. Keep the
                placeholders when translating. The count announced after every search goes through
                <code>ResultsAnnouncementFor(count)</code>, which picks <code>NoResultsAnnouncement</code>, <code>OneResultAnnouncement</code>
                or <code>ResultsAnnouncement</code>; a language whose plural forms one sentence per count cannot express - Polish, Russian,
                Arabic - overrides it, as the C# tab shows.
                <br />
                <code>NoResults</code> and <code>Empty</code> default to the library-wide <code>BitStrings.Current.NoResultsFound</code> and
                <code>NoItemsFound</code>, read each time they are shown: an app that already translates BlazorUI's own words through a
                <code>BitStrings</code> subclass (set as <code>BitStrings.Current</code>, or through
                <code>AddBitBlazorUIServices(options => options.Strings = ...)</code>) gets those two translated as well, unless the palette's
                strings set them. The <code>NoResultsText</code> and <code>EmptyText</code> parameters win over both.
                <br />
                When command titles are translated, give each command an explicit <code>Id</code> - an id derived from the title would change
                with the language, and the recents, the pins and the active row with it - and add the untranslated title as a keyword
                (<code>.Keywords("Open settings")</code> on <i>Einstellungen öffnen</i>), so people who learned the English name still find
                it, as VS Code does with its English aliases. Search ignores case and accents, and folds the Arabic and Persian forms of a
                letter and Persian and Arabic-Indic digits together (see the search sections above), so a query typed on another keyboard
                layout still matches.
                <br />
                Switch the language below. The placeholder, the footer's hints, the empty state (type <code>xyz</code>), the confirmation
                (choose the destructive row), the disabled reason and the back hint (open <i>Export…</i>), the switch's spoken state and the
                word between the key caps of <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> all change, and typing <code>settings</code> or <code>dark</code>
                finds the translated commands in every language. The ids stay the same, so the active row survives a switch.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    @foreach (var language in fLanguages)
                    {
                        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small"
                                   Variant="@(fLanguage == language ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                   OnClick="() => FSetLanguage(language)"
                                   lang="@language.Code">@language.Name</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <div lang="@fLanguage.Code">
                    <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                       IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                       EnableHistory="false"
                                       Strings="fLanguage.Strings"
                                       Commands="fL10nCommands" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="CSS variables" RazorCode="@example54RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example54CsharpCode" Id="example54">
            <div>
                The palette is drawn from BlazorUI's theme tokens, and the parts an app most often re-skins are CSS variables of its own.
                They are read with a fallback and never declared by the library, so a value set on <code>:root</code> in the app's
                stylesheet re-skins every palette, one set on an element re-skins the palettes inside it, and one set through a palette's
                <code>Style</code> re-skins that palette alone - with no selector of the library's to fight.
                <ul>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-width</code>: the modal dialog's width (640px; 920px with a detail pane). Never wider than
                        the viewport.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-max-height</code>: how tall the list grows with its header and footer
                        (<code>min(70vh, 560px)</code>).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-top</code>: how far down the modal dialog opens (12vh).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-radius</code>: the corners of the dialog or of an inline palette (the theme's dialog radius).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-background</code> and <code>--bit-CommandPalette-color</code>: the fill and the text
                        (the theme's primary background and foreground).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-border-color</code>: the lines between the header, the list and the footer, the edge of an
                        inline palette and of the detail pane.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-shadow</code> and <code>--bit-CommandPalette-backdrop</code>: the modal dialog's elevation
                        and the fill of the page behind it.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-row-height</code>: a row's least height (44px at the default density). While
                        <code>Virtualize</code> is on, the rows are pinned to <code>BitCommandPaletteOptions.RowHeight</code> instead.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-item-radius</code>: a row's corners.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-active-background</code>: the active row, the palette's only "focus" in the list.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-accent</code>: the mode chip, badges, checked rows, switches that are on, the selected
                        count and the busy spinner (the theme's primary color).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-mark-background</code>: the matched characters.</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-detail-width</code>: the detail pane (40%).</li>
                    <li><code>--bit-CommandPalette-focus-color</code>: the focus ring of the palette's buttons - back, breadcrumbs, close,
                        retry (the global focus ring).</li>
                </ul>
                <b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b> has its own: <code>--bit-CommandPaletteTrigger-height</code>, <code>-background</code>,
                <code>-color</code>, <code>-border-color</code>, <code>-radius</code> and <code>-focus-color</code>.
                <br />
                Everything else follows the theme, so it is changed there, for the whole app: the footer and the detail pane are on
                <code>--bit-clr-bg-sec</code>, subtitles and hints are <code>--bit-clr-fg-sec</code>, destructive rows and errors
                <code>--bit-clr-err</code> (on <code>--bit-clr-err-tint</code> when active), badges and the mode chip sit on
                <code>--bit-clr-pri-tint</code>, key caps are drawn in <code>--bit-clr-bg-sec</code> and <code>--bit-clr-brd-pri</code>,
                the type is <code>--bit-tpg-font-family</code>, the stacking order <code>--bit-zin-modal</code> and the pop-in
                <code>--bit-mot-duration</code>. Paddings, gaps and the heights of the search box and the footer follow the theme's spacing
                unit and density (see Density). A part no variable reaches is restyled through <code>Classes</code> / <code>Styles</code>
                (see Style &amp; Class).
                <br />
                Every color a re-skin sets has to hold up in both light and dark: a color derived from the theme's own tokens, as the sample
                does with <code>color-mix(in srgb, #ea580c 5%, var(--bit-clr-bg-pri))</code>, follows the theme by itself. Keep text at 4.5:1
                against its background and the active row distinct from the rest (3:1). The modal-only variables (width, top, shadow,
                backdrop) change nothing on an inline palette, which is as wide as its container and has an edge instead of a shadow: they
                apply to the page's modal palette the same way, set on <code>:root</code> or on its <code>Style</code>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Label="Search mail" Style="@fTriggerStyle" />
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fSkinCommands" Style="@fVariablesStyle">
                    <DetailTemplate Context="command">
                        <b>@command.Title</b>
                        <p>@(command.Subtitle ?? command.Group)</p>
                    </DetailTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Ready-made skins" RazorCode="@example55RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example55CsharpCode" Id="example55">
            <div>
                A skin is a set of the palette's CSS variables. Applied through <code>Style</code> it re-skins one palette; put in a class
                of the app's stylesheet and given through <code>Class</code>, or set on <code>:root</code>, it re-skins every palette that
                should look alike - the page's modal one included. The eight below are ready to copy. Every one derives its colors from the
                theme's tokens with <code>color-mix()</code> or leaves them to the theme, so each works in light and in dark: switch the
                site's theme to see them follow.
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Default</b>: no variables, the theme's own look.</li>
                    <li><b>Indigo</b>: a deep blue-violet accent with softer, rounder corners.</li>
                    <li><b>Teal</b> and <b>Rose</b>: an accent, with the surface, the edges and the active row tinted by it.</li>
                    <li><b>Flat</b>: square corners, no shadow and a stronger edge, for a palette laid into a dense page.</li>
                    <li><b>Pill</b>: round rows and corners, taller rows.</li>
                    <li><b>Dense list</b>: short rows in a short list, for a palette in a side panel.</li>
                    <li><b>Terminal</b>: a monospaced font and a green accent - a skin may set more than variables, here
                        <code>font-family</code> on the palette, which its parts inherit.</li>
                </ul>
                For a dark palette on a light page, or the other way round, do not hard-code dark colors in a skin: switch the theme of that
                palette (see Light &amp; dark), and the theme's tokens - and every skin built on them - follow.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    @foreach (var skin in fSkins)
                    {
                        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small"
                                   Variant="@(fSkin == skin ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                   OnClick="() => fSkin = skin">@skin.Name</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fSkinCommands" Style="@fSkin.Style" />
                <br />
                <BitText>Style: <code>@(fSkin.Style.Length == 0 ? "(none)" : fSkin.Style)</code></BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Light & dark" RazorCode="@example56RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example56CsharpCode" Id="example56">
            <div>
                The palette follows the app's theme: the light or dark <code>bit-theme</code> the app's <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> carries,
                which <code>BitThemeManager</code> sets - from the user's choice, or from the system's preference when the app follows it.
                Nothing has to be set on the palette for that.
                <br />
                To give one palette a theme of its own - a dark palette over a light page, or a light one in a dark side panel - set the
                theme on it or on an element around it:
                <ul>
                    <li>A <code>bit-theme="dark"</code> (or <code>"light"</code>) attribute on the palette itself: an attribute the palette
                        does not know lands on its root element, the modal <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> or the inline
                        <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>. BlazorUI's theme declares every palette of tokens (colors, shadows,
                        <code>color-scheme</code>) on <code>:root [bit-theme="dark"]</code> as well as on <code>:root[bit-theme="dark"]</code>,
                        so any element with the attribute re-themes itself and everything in it.</li>
                    <li>The same attribute on any ancestor: <code>&lt;aside bit-theme="dark"&gt;</code> re-themes the palette and every
                        other component in it.</li>
                    <li>A <b>BitThemeProvider</b> around the palette, with a <code>BitTheme</code> of its own - for instance
                        <code>BitThemeFactory.CreateDarkThemeFromSeed(BitAccentColorPresets.Teal)</code>, a whole dark palette derived from a
                        brand color - for a theme that is not one of the packaged ones.</li>
                </ul>
                The page's modal palette takes the attribute the same way:
                <code>&lt;BitCommandPalette bit-theme="dark" /&gt;</code>.
                <br />
                To change a palette variable in dark mode only, set it under both selectors the theme uses:
                <code>:root[bit-theme="dark"], :root [bit-theme="dark"] { --bit-CommandPalette-accent: #2dd4bf; }</code>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    @foreach (var (scheme, label) in fSchemes)
                    {
                        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small"
                                   Variant="@(fScheme == scheme ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                   OnClick="() => fScheme = scheme">@label</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fSkinCommands" bit-theme="@fScheme" />
                <br />
                <BitText>A palette inside a BitThemeProvider with a dark theme of its own, teal:</BitText>
                <br />
                <BitThemeProvider Theme="fDarkTheme">
                    <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fSkinCommands" />
                </BitThemeProvider>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Density" RazorCode="@example57RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example57CsharpCode" Id="example57">
            <div>
                The palette's paddings, gaps and heights are multiples of the theme's spacing unit scaled by
                <code>--bit-layout-density-scale</code>, a unitless number (1 by default). Set it on the palette's <code>Style</code>, or on
                any element around it, and the palette tightens or loosens: the rows' padding and least height, the list's padding, the
                group headings, the search box's row, the breadcrumbs, the footer, the empty and loading states, the detail pane's padding,
                the key caps and the badges. Font sizes, icons and corner radii stay as they are, so text keeps its size. Below 1 is
                compact (0.8 suits a dense app or an inline palette in a side panel), above 1 is airy (1.2 for touch).
                <br />
                The scale is read where each part is drawn, so it reaches every BlazorUI component under the element it is set on: a
                <code>BitThemeProvider</code> with <code>BitThemeDensityPresets.CreateCompactOverlay()</code> (0.9) or
                <code>CreateSpaciousOverlay()</code> (1.1), or a <code>BitTheme</code> whose <code>Layout.DensityScale</code> is set, makes a
                whole region denser, palette included. <code>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</code> scales the same way, as the triggers below do.
                <br />
                A compact palette is <code>Style="--bit-layout-density-scale: 0.8"</code>. A density of your own is a class: one that sets the scale and, where
                the scale is not enough, the palette's variables, such as
                <code>.cozy { --bit-layout-density-scale: 0.9; --bit-CommandPalette-row-height: 38px; }</code> given through
                <code>Class</code>. A <code>--bit-CommandPalette-row-height</code> that is set wins over the scaled row height; with
                <code>Virtualize</code> on, the rows are pinned to <code>BitCommandPaletteOptions.RowHeight</code>, which should then match
                the density.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    @foreach (var (name, scale) in fDensities)
                    {
                        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Small"
                                   Variant="@(fDensity == scale ? BitVariant.Fill : BitVariant.Outline)"
                                   OnClick="() => fDensity = scale">@name (@scale)</BitButton>
                    }
                </div>
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Style="@($"--bit-layout-density-scale: {fDensity}")" />
                </div>
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="fSkinCommands"
                                   Style="@($"--bit-layout-density-scale: {fDensity}")" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Forced colors, contrast & motion" RazorCode="@example58RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example58CsharpCode" Id="example58">
            <div>
                The palette follows what the reader asked of their system, with nothing to set:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Forced colors</b> (Windows High Contrast and other forced palettes): backgrounds and shadows disappear in that
                        mode, so the dialog gets a border, the header, the footer, the breadcrumbs, the badges and the key caps are outlined,
                        the active row is outlined in the system's <code>Highlight</code> color, a disabled row is drawn in
                        <code>GrayText</code> instead of being faded, checked boxes and switches that are on are filled with
                        <code>Highlight</code>, and matched characters are drawn as <code>Mark</code> / <code>MarkText</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>More contrast</b> (<code>prefers-contrast: more</code>): the active row is outlined as well as filled, matched
                        characters are underlined as well as tinted, and BlazorUI's theme draws borders twice as thick, focus rings wider and
                        the faintest text and lines one step stronger.</li>
                    <li><b>Reduced motion</b> (<code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>): the dialog's pop-in, the backdrop's fade and the
                        switches' slide are dropped, as every duration of the theme collapses; the spinners keep turning - they are the only
                        sign that work is in progress - but slowly and evenly.</li>
                    <li><b>Small screens</b> (below 600px): the modal palette fills the screen, the footer keeps only its last hint, and the
                        detail pane and the rows' shortcuts are hidden. An inline palette keeps its place in the page.</li>
                    <li><b>Right to left</b>: see RTL below.</li>
                </ul>
                The default colors are the theme's, whose text pairs meet WCAG AA (4.5:1), on the active row and the footer too; a re-skin
                should keep them there. To try the modes below without changing the system, emulate them in the browser's developer tools
                (Chrome: Rendering › Emulate CSS media feature <code>forced-colors</code>, <code>prefers-contrast</code>,
                <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>) and move through the list: the switch, the badge, the destructive row (press Enter on it
                once), the disabled row and the matched characters all change.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" Commands="fSkinCommands" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Restyling from scratch" RazorCode="@example59RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example59CsharpCode" Id="example59">
            <div>
                <code>BitCommandPalette</code>'s styles are part of the Extras bundle like every other component's, and the
                palette is restyled on top of them, from the lightest touch to a look of its own:
                <ul>
                    <li>The CSS variables, for its colors and sizes (see CSS variables and Ready-made skins).</li>
                    <li><code>Class</code> and <code>Style</code> on the root, and <code>Classes</code> / <code>Styles</code> on its parts: the
                        header, the search box, the list, the group headings, the rows, the active row, the states, the footer and the
                        detail pane (see Style &amp; Class).</li>
                    <li>The templates, to replace what a row, a heading, the icon, the header, the footer and the states show.</li>
                    <li>The stable <code>data-bit-cpl</code> attributes, as selectors under a class of your own, for the parts that have no
                        <code>Classes</code> entry.</li>
                </ul>
                The <code>data-bit-cpl</code> attributes name every part: <code>dialog</code> (<code>inline</code> for an inline palette),
                <code>root</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>breadcrumbs</code>, <code>crumb</code>, <code>mode</code>,
                <code>input-row</code>, <code>input</code>, <code>spinner</code>, <code>selected-count</code>, <code>close</code>,
                <code>notice</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>group</code>, <code>group-heading</code>, <code>item</code>, <code>path</code>,
                <code>label</code>, <code>mark</code>, <code>badge</code>, <code>shortcut</code>, <code>empty</code> (no results and empty
                alike), <code>loading</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>retry</code>, <code>prompt</code>, <code>prompt-error</code>,
                <code>footer</code>, <code>toast</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>alert</code>, <code>detail</code>, <code>sequence</code>
                (the pending-sequence hint) and <code>trigger</code> on BitCommandPaletteTrigger. A row carries its state as attributes:
                <code>data-active</code>, <code>data-checked</code>, <code>data-disabled</code>, <code>data-destructive</code>,
                <code>data-confirming</code>, <code>data-running</code> and <code>data-error</code> (all <code>"true"</code> when set),
                <code>data-kind</code> (the command's kind) and <code>data-value</code> (its id). The root carries <code>data-open</code>,
                <code>data-busy</code>, <code>data-multiselect</code>, <code>data-has-detail</code> and <code>data-ready</code> - set once
                its browser layer listens for keys, which is what an end-to-end test waits for before pressing Ctrl+K on a prerendered
                page. They are also the stable hooks for tests; the <code>bit-cpl-*</code> classes may change between versions.
                <br />
                The palette below is styled by a handful of rules under its own class: no rounded rows, an inverted active row,
                underlined matches, struck-through unavailable rows and no footer.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   Class="f-pantry"
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Placeholder="Search the pantry…"
                                   Commands="fPantry" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example60RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example60CsharpCode" Id="example60">
            <div>
                The palette follows the WAI-ARIA combobox pattern with a grouped listbox. What that means for a page that restyles it or
                fills its templates:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>The focus stays in the search box.</b> Rows are never focused: the box points at the active row through
                        <code>aria-activedescendant</code>, and a click on a row leaves the focus in the box. The active row's fill
                        (<code>--bit-CommandPalette-active-background</code>) is therefore the only sign of where the keyboard is: keep it
                        clearly distinct from the background. A template must not put a button, a link or any other focusable element in a
                        row - it could never be reached with the keyboard.</li>
                    <li><b>The row's state is the palette's.</b> The row element keeps <code>aria-selected</code> (or
                        <code>aria-checked</code> on a multi-select page) and <code>aria-disabled</code>, and the reason a row is disabled is
                        said in its subtitle. A switch's state and a pin are spoken by the default row in words (<code>Strings.On</code>,
                        <code>Strings.Off</code>, <code>Strings.Pinned</code>), not only drawn; an <code>ItemTemplate</code> replaces them, so it
                        says them itself, in visually hidden text, as the sample below does.</li>
                    <li><b>Groups are named by their headings</b>, which are presentational: a <code>GroupHeaderTemplate</code>'s text is
                        what a screen reader says when it enters the group.</li>
                    <li><b>Live regions say the rest</b>: polite regions, present before anything is announced in them, read the result count
                        after every search, the page the palette moved to ("Theme. 3 results available"), the loading state and every toast,
                        whatever the footer shows; errors are announced assertively. An inline palette announces only while it has the focus.
                        A template therefore adds no <code>aria-live</code> or <code>role="status"</code> for these - except a
                        <code>LoadingTemplate</code>, which replaces a state that had one.</li>
                    <li><b>Key caps are hidden from screen readers</b> and the shortcut is spoken in words: draw shortcuts with
                        <code>BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint</code> rather than bare <code>&lt;kbd&gt;</code>s, or hide yours.</li>
                    <li><b>The pointer</b> moves the active row only once it actually moves, so a list scrolling under a resting mouse does not
                        steal the keyboard's place. <b>DisablePointerSelection</b> stops hovering from moving it at all - only the keys and a
                        click do - as in the palette below.</li>
                    <li><b>Matched characters</b> keep the text's own color on their tint: a muted color on the tint would fall below 4.5:1.
                        Keep that when restyling <code>[data-bit-cpl="mark"]</code>.</li>
                    <li><b>The buttons</b> (back, breadcrumbs, close, retry) are at least 24px, and their names hold what they show: the close
                        button is "Close, Esc", and the trigger's name starts with its visible label (WCAG 2.5.3, label in name).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline IncludeRegisteredCommands="false" EnableHistory="false" DisablePointerSelection Commands="fSkinCommands">
                    <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                        <span class="f-row">
                            <span class="f-text">
                                <span class="f-title">
                                    @if (item.IsConfirming)
                                    {
                                        <b>Confirm: </b>
                                    }
                                    <BitCommandPaletteHighlightedText Text="@item.Row.Command.Title" Positions="@item.Row.Match?.TitlePositions" />
                                </span>
                                @if (item.Row.Command.Disabled && item.Row.Command.DisabledReason is not null)
                                {
                                    <span class="f-sub">@item.Row.Command.DisabledReason</span>
                                }
                            </span>
                            @if (item.Row.Command.Checked is { } on)
                            {
                                <span class="f-switch" aria-hidden="true">@(on ? "●" : "○")</span>
                                <span class="f-vhd">@(on ? "On" : "Off")</span>
                            }
                            @if (item.Row.Command.Shortcut is not null)
                            {
                                <BitCommandPaletteShortcutHint Shortcut="item.Row.Command.Shortcut" MacKeys="item.IsMac" />
                            }
                        </span>
                    </ItemTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Accessibility of the dialog and the list" RazorCode="@example61RazorCode" Id="example61">
            <div>
                The palette follows the WAI-ARIA patterns for a modal dialog and a combobox with a grouped listbox, so it works with the keyboard alone and with screen readers, without any setup.
                <ul>
                    <li>The modal palette is a native modal dialog element: it sits in the top layer, traps the focus, makes the page behind it inert, holds the page's scroll still, and gives the focus back to whatever had it when it closes.</li>
                    <li>The focus stays in the search input, which is a combobox controlling a listbox, and aria-activedescendant points at the active option, so screen readers follow the selection without the focus moving.</li>
                    <li>A single-select list marks its active option aria-selected; a multi-select page adds aria-multiselectable and marks its checked options aria-checked and nothing else, so each list has one state.</li>
                    <li>Each group is a role="group" named by its heading, so screen readers say which group an option is in, and the listbox owns only options and groups.</li>
                    <li>The list is a listbox only while it lists something; the empty, loading and error states are statuses and alerts instead, and the combobox's aria-expanded follows.</li>
                    <li>On a prompt the input is a plain text field named by the prompt's question.</li>
                    <li>In a virtualized list, where rows cannot be wrapped in their groups, each option is described by its heading and carries aria-posinset and aria-setsize.</li>
                    <li>Polite live regions, present before anything is announced in them, say the result count after every search, the page the palette moved to, loading, and every toast; errors are announced assertively, and the list is aria-busy while it loads.</li>
                    <li>A toggle's state and a pinned command are spoken ("On", "Off", "Pinned"), not only drawn.</li>
                    <li>A shortcut hint has an accessible name in words ("Ctrl+K", "G then S") while its key caps are hidden from screen readers, and the trigger declares aria-keyshortcuts.</li>
                    <li>Disabled commands are aria-disabled and say why, and group headings are not read as options.</li>
                    <li>Clicking a row leaves the focus in the input.</li>
                    <li>Pointer hover moves the selection only once the pointer actually moves, so a list scrolling under a resting mouse does not steal the keyboard's selection; DisablePointerSelection turns hover off completely.</li>
                    <li>The close button's accessible name holds its visible "Esc", and the trigger's name starts with its visible label (WCAG 2.5.3, label in name), as the Trigger section shows; the buttons are at least 24 pixels.</li>
                    <li>Animations follow BlazorUI's motion tokens, which collapse under prefers-reduced-motion.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>Open the palette with the trigger below and a screen reader on: the result count, the active option and its group are announced as you type and move.</BitText>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPaletteTrigger />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Diagnostics" RazorCode="@example62RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example62CsharpCode" Id="example62">
            <div>
                The palette reports what it does through System.Diagnostics, so OpenTelemetry, Bit.Belemetry or any MeterListener picks it up - and it records nothing while nobody listens. Its meter and its activity source are both named Bit.BlazorUI.CommandPalette, available as <b>BitCommandPaletteDiagnostics.MeterName</b> and <b>BitCommandPaletteDiagnostics.ActivitySourceName</b>.
                <ul>
                    <li>The bit.command_palette.command.executions counter (unit {execution}) counts the commands the palette ran, refused, or saw fail or be cancelled, tagged with bit.command_palette.command.kind, bit.command_palette.trigger (palette, shortcut or programmatic) and bit.command_palette.outcome (succeeded, failed, cancelled or refused).</li>
                    <li>The bit.command_palette.command.duration histogram (in seconds) records how long handlers took, with the same tags; a refusal ran nothing and has no duration.</li>
                    <li>The bit.command_palette.source.duration histogram (in seconds) records how long async sources took, tagged with bit.command_palette.source.name and bit.command_palette.outcome (succeeded, failed or timed_out); a search the palette abandoned is not recorded.</li>
                    <li>The bit.command_palette.search.duration histogram (in seconds) records how long matching and ranking the in-memory commands took for a query, with no tags.</li>
                    <li>Every handler runs inside a bit.command_palette.command activity, so the HttpClient calls it makes are its children, tagged with the kind, the trigger, the outcome and the command's id.</li>
                    <li>A failure sets the activity's status to an error and records the exception's type, never its message, which can quote what the user typed.</li>
                    <li>Every source search runs inside a bit.command_palette.source activity.</li>
                    <li>The palette's own rows (pin, forget, retry, "Show more") are neither counted nor traced.</li>
                    <li>Metrics never carry ids, and queries, prompt values, titles and selections are never recorded.</li>
                    <li>Set options.RecordCommandIdsInTraces to false when command ids embed personal data (person:ada@@example.com); it also keeps a command's id and title out of the log entries about running it, which read "(hidden)" instead.</li>
                    <li>On WebAssembly, metrics need the System.Diagnostics.Metrics feature switch (MetricsSupport), which the SDK turns off by default and Bit.Belemetry turns back on.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>Every command run in the samples on this page is counted and traced under Bit.BlazorUI.CommandPalette when the app has a listener.</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Troubleshooting" RazorCode="@example63RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example63CsharpCode" Id="example63">
            <div>
                Most problems come from the setup; the palette also logs a warning or an entry for most of what it refuses to do, so the app's log is the first place to look.
                <ul>
                    <li>When Ctrl+K does nothing, the palette is not interactive because it is in a statically rendered layout; also check that bit.blazorui.extras.js is linked and that no other script calls preventDefault on the key first.</li>
                    <li>When options from Program.cs (a toggle shortcut, the group order, commands added through options.Commands) stop working once the page turns interactive, the services are registered in only one project of a Blazor Web App and the palette in the other runs on the default options; it logs a warning saying the services are not registered. Call AddBitBlazorUIExtrasServices in both projects, through one shared method.</li>
                    <li>When the palette opens unstyled, the bit.blazorui.extras.css link is missing from the host page.</li>
                    <li>When a toggle shows its old state, remember that commands are immutable: register a new command with the same id, call Update on the service, or give BitCommandPaletteCommands a new list.</li>
                    <li>When recents forget a command whose title changed, its id was derived from the title; set an explicit Id.</li>
                    <li>When a page's commands are still listed after leaving it, dispose the handle Register returned, or register them with BitCommandPaletteCommands.</li>
                    <li>When a global shortcut does not fire in a text box, that is deliberate for plain keys; use a chord with a modifier, or set SuppressShortcutsInEditableElements to false.</li>
                    <li>When two palettes open at once, there is more than one modal palette in a scope; keep one, since every instance answers the same service, and use Inline for a palette that is part of a page.</li>
                    <li>When a command does nothing, or the wrong one runs, look at the app's log: the palette warns about duplicate ids, about shortcuts several commands claim or the toggle takes, and about links it refused, and it logs every failed command and page.</li>
                    <li>When a command does nothing and the footer says "You can’t run …", a guard refused it; the log has an information entry naming the command and what started it, and IBitCommandPaletteService.CommandRefused reports it to code.</li>
                    <li>When a command's shortcut works only after opening the palette, its VisibleWhen changed while the palette was closed; call Refresh() on the service after the state it reads changes.</li>
                    <li>When commands passed to the Commands parameter do not update, remember that the palette re-reads the parameter only when it gets a new collection; assign a new list, or call Refresh() after changing the same one.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitText>The palette on this page is set up as the Getting started section shows; compare your app's setup with it first.</BitText>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example64RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example64CsharpCode" Id="example64">
            <div>
                <b>Class</b> and <b>Style</b> go on the palette's root element - the modal <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, or the
                <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> of an inline palette - next to its own class, so a CSS variable set there reaches the whole
                palette. <b>Classes</b> and <b>Styles</b>, both a <b>BitCommandPaletteClassStyles</b>, reach its parts:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>Root</code>: the root element, like <code>Class</code> / <code>Style</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>Header</code>: the header, with the breadcrumbs and the search box (not a <code>HeaderTemplate</code>, which is
                        rendered above it).</li>
                    <li><code>Input</code>: the search box.</li>
                    <li><code>List</code>: the list.</li>
                    <li><code>GroupHeading</code>: every group heading.</li>
                    <li><code>Item</code>: every row; <code>ActiveItem</code>: the active row, added to <code>Item</code> (its style is written
                        after <code>Item</code>'s, so it wins).</li>
                    <li><code>Empty</code>: what a page with nothing on it shows; <code>NoResults</code>: what a search that matched nothing
                        shows (around their templates too).</li>
                    <li><code>Footer</code>: the footer (not a <code>FooterTemplate</code>, which replaces it).</li>
                    <li><code>Detail</code>: the detail pane, when a <code>DetailTemplate</code> is set.</li>
                </ul>
                A style in <code>Styles</code> is written on the element, so it wins over the stylesheet. A class in <code>Classes</code> is
                matched against the palette's own rules, so a rule that changes what the palette already sets for a state - the active row's
                fill, say - needs a selector at least as specific (<code>.my-item[data-active="true"]</code>), or the variable that is meant
                for it (<code>--bit-CommandPalette-active-background</code>). Open <i>Nothing inside</i> for the <code>Empty</code> part and
                type <code>zz</code> for <code>NoResults</code>.
                <br />
                <b>BitCommandPaletteTrigger</b> has its own <code>Class</code> / <code>Style</code> and <code>Classes</code> /
                <code>Styles</code>, a <b>BitCommandPaletteTriggerClassStyles</b>: <code>Root</code> (the button), <code>Icon</code> (the
                search icon), <code>Label</code> and <code>Shortcut</code> (the key caps). A trigger opens the page's modal palette.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="fColorCommands"
                                   Class="f-custom-class"
                                   Style="--bit-CommandPalette-accent: #7c3aed; --bit-CommandPalette-active-background: #7c3aed22;" />
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="fColorCommands"
                                   Styles="@(new() { Root = "border-color: #7c3aed;",
                                                     Header = "background: #7c3aed14;",
                                                     Input = "font-weight: 600;",
                                                     List = "padding: 0.75rem;",
                                                     GroupHeading = "color: #7c3aed; text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0;",
                                                     Item = "margin-block: 2px;",
                                                     ActiveItem = "background: #7c3aed; color: white;",
                                                     Empty = "color: #7c3aed;",
                                                     NoResults = "color: #b91c1c;",
                                                     Footer = "background: #7c3aed14;",
                                                     Detail = "background: #7c3aed0d;" })">
                    <DetailTemplate Context="command">
                        <b>@command.Title</b>
                        <p>@command.Description</p>
                    </DetailTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <BitCommandPalette Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Commands="fColorCommands"
                                   Classes="@(new() { Root = "f-root",
                                                      Header = "f-header-part",
                                                      Input = "f-input",
                                                      List = "f-list",
                                                      GroupHeading = "f-heading",
                                                      Item = "f-item",
                                                      ActiveItem = "f-active-item",
                                                      Empty = "f-empty",
                                                      NoResults = "f-no-results",
                                                      Footer = "f-footer-part",
                                                      Detail = "f-detail-part" })">
                    <DetailTemplate Context="command">
                        <b>@command.Title</b>
                        <p>@command.Description</p>
                    </DetailTemplate>
                </BitCommandPalette>
                <br />
                <div class="f-buttons">
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Styles="@(new() { Root = "border-color: #7c3aed; border-radius: 999px;",
                                                                Icon = "color: #7c3aed;",
                                                                Label = "font-weight: 600;",
                                                                Shortcut = "opacity: .7;" })" />
                    <BitCommandPaletteTrigger Classes="@(new() { Root = "f-trigger",
                                                                 Icon = "f-trigger-icon",
                                                                 Label = "f-trigger-label",
                                                                 Shortcut = "f-trigger-shortcut" })" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example65RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example65CsharpCode" Id="example65">
            <div>
                Use BitCommandPalette in right-to-left (RTL). <code>Dir="BitDir.Rtl"</code> lays the palette out from the right; so does a
                <code>dir="rtl"</code> on any element around it (prefer <code>Dir</code> on the palette: the few browsers without the
                <code>:dir()</code> selector mirror its arrows only then). Every rule uses logical properties, so:
                <ul>
                    <li>The search icon, the rows' icons and the back button start on the right; the footer's last hint, the detail pane and
                        its border, and a switch's knob (on at the left) all follow.</li>
                    <li>Arrows and chevrons are mirrored - the back arrow, a page's chevron, the arrow of a navigation command - and the
                        separator between a nested command's ancestors turns into ‹ ("خروجی گرفتن… ‹ به صورت PDF": type <code>PDF</code>).</li>
                    <li>User text - titles, subtitles, badges, values, breadcrumbs, headings - isolates its own direction
                        (<code>dir="auto"</code>), so an English title in a Persian palette, such as <i>Open in VS Code…</i> below, keeps its
                        punctuation where it belongs. Shortcut key caps always read left to right, as on the keys:
                        <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd>, with <code>ShortcutThen</code> between them.</li>
                    <li><b>ArrowLeft and ArrowRight trade places.</b> On an empty search box the palette reads the arrow pointing to the start
                        of the line as "back" and the one pointing to its end as "into the action panel". In right-to-left text the start is on
                        the right, so <kbd>→</kbd> goes back a page and <kbd>←</kbd> opens the action panel of the active row. Open
                        <i>خروجی گرفتن…</i> and press <kbd>→</kbd> to come back; on <i>باز کردن تنظیمات</i>, press <kbd>←</kbd> for its
                        action. Backspace on an empty box and Escape go back in either direction.</li>
                </ul>
                The words are the palette's <code>Strings</code> (see Localization): the sample translates every one of them into Persian,
                and overrides <code>ResultsAnnouncementFor</code> to announce the count in Persian digits (۱۲), which a screen reader reading
                Persian expects. Search folds the Arabic and Persian forms of a letter and the Persian, Arabic-Indic and Latin digits
                together, so <code>2</code> and <code>۲</code> both find the export's subtitle, and a shortcut such as <kbd>G</kbd>
                <kbd>S</kbd> works when a Persian keyboard layout types ل س (see the keyboard sections above). In Persian and Arabic,
                <code>TriggerNameSeparator</code> is "، ".
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitCommandPalette Dir="BitDir.Rtl"
                                   lang="fa"
                                   Inline
                                   IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                                   EnableHistory="false"
                                   Strings="fPersianStrings"
                                   Commands="fRtlCommands" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
